window.p3Answers=[{"b":"02","c":0,"i":0,"en":"I'd say yes, but with one condition — it should be done lightly. Children naturally love stories about kings, castles and old adventures, so if history is introduced as storytelling rather than dates and exams, it works really well. The problem starts when schools turn it into pure memorisation too early, because then kids switch off before they've even had a chance to enjoy it.","cn":"我觉得应该，但有个前提——要轻量地做。孩子天生就喜欢国王、城堡、老冒险这类故事，所以如果把历史当成讲故事来引入，而不是年份和考试，效果会很好。问题出在学校太早把它变成纯粹的背诵，孩子还没来得及喜欢上就已经关掉兴趣了。"},{"b":"02","c":0,"i":1,"en":"Around seven or eight, when they start primary school, seems about right for formal learning. But informally they can start much earlier — from about four or five — through picture books, cartoons and family stories. So the age really depends on what you mean by 'learning'; as a fun thing, earlier is better, as a school subject, seven or eight is fine.","cn":"大概七八岁开始上小学时，开始正式学习比较合适。但非正式的话，其实可以更早——四五岁起就能通过绘本、动画片和家里的故事接触。所以关键看你怎么定义「学习」：当成好玩的事，越早越好；当成学校科目，七八岁就可以了。"},{"b":"02","c":0,"i":2,"en":"Mostly through stories and play, not textbooks. Grandparents are often the main teachers — they tell tales about what life used to be like. Kids also pick things up from picture books, cartoons, museum visits and traditional festivals. At that age, they remember feelings and characters far better than facts, so the emotional side is what matters.","cn":"主要是通过故事和玩耍，而不是课本。爷爷奶奶往往是主要的老师——他们会讲过去的生活是什么样的。孩子也会从绘本、动画片、博物馆参观和传统节日里学到东西。那个年纪，他们对感受和人物的记忆远好过事实，所以情感部分是关键。"},{"b":"02","c":0,"i":3,"en":"Yes, I think it's genuinely important. When you learn how people in the past cooked, worked, travelled or raised families, you realise that a lot of things we take for granted are actually recent inventions. It builds empathy and makes you less arrogant about your own era. It also helps you understand why modern problems exist, because most of them have roots in the past.","cn":"我觉得确实很重要。当你了解到过去的人怎么做饭、工作、出行、养家，你会发现我们习以为常的很多东西其实都是近代才有的。这能培养共情，也让你对自己的时代没那么自大。它还能帮你理解现代问题为什么存在，因为大多数问题的根源都在过去。"},{"b":"02","c":0,"i":4,"en":"Personally I'd say no, it's better to mix them. Starting with ancient history can feel remote and abstract — talking about a dynasty three thousand years ago can bore a child. Modern history is closer to their own lives; they can see the buildings and talk to people who lived through it. That said, ancient history gives useful context, so a sensible approach is to move back and forth rather than strictly in order.","cn":"我个人觉得不必，最好是穿插着学。从古代史开始会显得遥远抽象——讲三千年前的朝代容易让孩子觉得无聊。近现代史更贴近他们的生活，能看到那些建筑，也能跟经历过的人聊。不过古代史也提供了有用的背景，所以理性的做法是来回交替，而不是严格按顺序。"},{"b":"02","c":0,"i":5,"en":"Two things stand out for me. The first is everyday life — what ordinary people ate, wore and worried about, because that's what really shows you how a society worked. The second is the human side: the mistakes, the coincidences and the personal choices behind big events. History isn't just a list of battles and treaties; it's people, and that's what makes it memorable and useful.","cn":"有两点我觉得特别重要。第一是日常生活——普通人吃什么、穿什么、担心什么，因为那才真正反映一个社会是怎么运转的。第二是人的一面：重大事件背后的失误、巧合和个人选择。历史不只是战役和条约的清单，它是关于人的，这才让它既有记忆点又有用。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I'd say they're important, though not because everyone will become an artist. Art classes train things that other subjects don't — patience, observation and the confidence to try something without a fixed answer. For a lot of children, art is also the one lesson where there's no wrong result, and that can be a huge relief if they struggle academically.","cn":"我觉得挺重要的，倒不是因为每个人都会成为艺术家。美术课训练的是其他科目不练的东西——耐心、观察力，还有在没有标准答案的情况下敢于尝试的信心。对很多孩子来说，美术还是唯一一门没有「错」的课，如果他们在学业上吃力，这会是很重要的放松。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":1,"en":"Mostly because it's hands-on and the result is immediate. You can see what you made at the end of the lesson, which feels satisfying. There's also something calming about working with your hands — it's a break from screens and from being told what to do. And for shyer children, making things is a way to express themselves without having to speak up in front of the class.","cn":"主要是因为动手做、又有即时成果。下课时能看到自己做出来的东西，很有成就感。动手也会让人放松——能离开屏幕，也不用听别人指挥。对比较内向的孩子来说，做手工是一种不用在全班面前讲话就能表达自己的方式。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":2,"en":"To be honest, less than before. Most children now would rather play games on a tablet than cut and glue paper, simply because screens give faster rewards. But it hasn't disappeared — crafts are still popular when they're tied to something fun, like making decorations for a festival or a gift for a parent. So it depends a lot on how it's presented.","cn":"老实说，不如以前多了。现在大多数孩子宁愿在平板上打游戏，也不愿意剪纸、粘胶水，因为屏幕给的反馈更快。但它也没消失——当手工和好玩的事情挂上钩，比如过节做装饰、给爸妈做礼物，还是很受欢迎的。所以很大程度上看怎么引导。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":3,"en":"Quite a big impact, actually. A well-designed building isn't just a place to live or work — it shapes how people feel every day. Landmark buildings can become symbols of a city and give residents a sense of pride and identity. On the other hand, ugly or badly planned buildings can make an area feel depressing, so architecture really does influence the mood of a whole community.","cn":"影响其实挺大的。设计好的建筑不只是居住或工作的场所，它每天都在影响人的感受。地标建筑能成为城市的象征，让居民产生自豪感和认同感。反过来，难看或规划糟糕的建筑会让整个区域显得压抑，所以建筑确实会影响整个社区的情绪。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":4,"en":"Yes, definitely. Creative people tend to look at problems from unusual angles, which matters when the team is stuck. They also come up with options that more conventional thinkers would never consider. Of course, a team of only creatives can be chaotic, so the ideal is a mix — creative people to generate ideas and practical people to turn them into something workable.","cn":"当然好。有创意的人习惯从不同角度看问题，这在团队卡住的时候特别关键。他们还能提出思维比较常规的人根本想不到的方案。当然，一个全是创意型人才的团队可能会很乱，所以最理想的是搭配——创意的人出点子，务实的人把它变成可执行的东西。"},{"b":"02","c":1,"i":5,"en":"Yes, I believe art has a real effect on society. Music, theatre and film can bring people together, start conversations about difficult issues and give a voice to groups who are usually ignored. They also preserve culture and history in a way that textbooks can't. Plus, on a personal level, art is one of the main ways people cope with stress and express emotions, and that benefits society as a whole.","cn":"我认为艺术对社会确实有实际影响。音乐、戏剧和电影能把人聚在一起，引发关于棘手话题的讨论，也能为常被忽视的群体发声。它们还能以课本做不到的方式保存文化和历史。另外从个人层面讲，艺术是人们缓解压力、表达情绪的主要方式之一，这对整个社会都是有益的。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":0,"en":"Quite a lot, actually. Young people can teach the elderly how to use phones, apps and online services, which makes their daily life much easier. In return, older people pass on experience — how to handle a difficult boss, how to save money, how to stay calm when things go wrong. I think both sides benefit as long as they're actually willing to listen to each other.","cn":"其实挺多的。年轻人能教老人用手机、App和各种线上服务，让日常生活方便很多。反过来，老人传授经验——怎么应对难缠的上司、怎么攒钱、出事时怎么保持冷静。只要双方真的愿意听对方说，我觉得是双赢的。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":1,"en":"Yes, definitely improved. Pensions are more reliable now, medical care is much better, and many old people have heating, smartphones and even travel abroad, which was unthinkable for their parents. That said, there's a downside — they live longer but often alone, because children move to big cities for work. So materially it's better, emotionally it can be lonelier than before.","cn":"确实改善了。现在养老金更可靠，医疗好得多，很多老人家里有暖气、用智能手机，甚至能出国旅游，这在他们的父辈是不可想象的。不过也有另一面——他们活得更久，但常常独居，因为子女都去大城市工作了。所以物质上更好了，情感上可能比以前更孤独。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":2,"en":"I think it comes down to two things: speed of change and different reference points. Young people grow up with the internet, so they adapt to new things instantly, while older people rely on habits formed decades ago. They also grew up in completely different social and economic conditions, so their values about work, money and family can clash. It's not really about age itself — it's about the world they each grew up in.","cn":"我觉得主要是两点：变化的速度和参照物不同。年轻人从小接触互联网，对新事物适应得快；老人则依赖几十年前形成的习惯。而且他们成长的社会和经济环境完全不同，在关于工作、金钱和家庭的价值观上就容易冲突。其实关键不在年龄本身，而在于各自成长的那个世界。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":3,"en":"It varies a lot. The active ones do tai chi or square dancing in the park early in the morning, then shop for groceries and cook. Many look after grandchildren, which is basically a full-time job. Others spend their time playing chess, watching TV dramas or chatting with neighbours. And a growing number are learning new things — some of my mum's friends take painting classes and even use short-video apps.","cn":"差别挺大的。活跃的老人早上在公园打太极或跳广场舞，然后买菜做饭。很多人要带孙辈，基本等于全职工作。也有人下棋、追剧或者和邻居聊天。还有越来越多的老人在学新东西——我妈的一些朋友在上绘画课，甚至玩短视频。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":4,"en":"The most common ones are low-impact: walking, tai chi and square dancing, because they're easy on the joints and can be done in groups. Swimming is also popular since it's gentle. In recent years, more older people have taken up things like badminton and cycling. Competitive sports are less common, mainly because of injury risk, so the focus tends to be on staying healthy and socialising rather than winning.","cn":"最常见的是低强度运动：散步、太极、广场舞，因为对关节友好，又能一群一群地做。游泳也很受欢迎，因为比较温和。近几年越来越多老人开始打羽毛球、骑车。竞技性运动相对少，主要是怕受伤，所以重点更多在保持健康和社交，而不是争输赢。"},{"b":"02","c":2,"i":5,"en":"I'd say they remember things that were emotionally strong — weddings, the birth of a child, their first job, or difficult periods like shortages in the past. Interestingly, they often remember childhood details vividly while forgetting what they ate yesterday. I think that's because strong emotions fix memories much more firmly than routine events do, and early experiences are usually the most intense.","cn":"我觉得他们记得的是情感强烈的事——婚礼、孩子出生、第一份工作，或者过去物资匮乏那样的艰难时期。有意思的是，他们常常清楚记得童年的细节，却忘了昨天吃了什么。我想是因为强烈的情感比日常琐事更牢固地把记忆固定下来，而早年的经历往往最强烈。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":0,"en":"Mainly because life keeps changing. Learning a new skill builds confidence and makes you less afraid of the unknown, so when a job requires something new you don't panic. It also teaches patience — you go through the stage of being bad at something, which is uncomfortable but useful. And practically, children who learn early tend to keep learning as adults rather than avoiding anything unfamiliar.","cn":"主要是因为生活一直在变。学新技能能建立自信，让人不那么怕未知，所以工作需要新东西时你不会慌。它还能教会耐心——你要经历「很糟糕」的阶段，虽然难受但很有用。而且从实际角度看，从小会学的孩子长大后往往继续保持学习习惯，而不是躲避陌生事物。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":1,"en":"There are lots of places now. Schools cover the basics, but outside that you have private classes, sports clubs, community centres and online courses. The internet is probably the biggest change — a child can learn coding, a musical instrument or a language from free videos at home. And of course, a lot happens informally at home, just by watching and copying what parents or older siblings do.","cn":"现在渠道很多。学校教基础的，此外还有课外班、体育俱乐部、社区中心和网课。互联网大概是最大的变化——孩子在家就能靠免费视频学编程、乐器或者语言。当然，家里也有很多非正式的学习，就是看和模仿父母或哥哥姐姐怎么做。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":2,"en":"I think the key is breaking it down and showing rather than just telling. A good teacher or parent demonstrates the step, lets the child try, then gives feedback instead of taking over. Praise matters too, but it should be specific — 'you held the pencil better this time' works better than a vague 'well done'. And most importantly, adults need patience, because rushing a child usually kills their interest.","cn":"我觉得关键是拆解步骤，并且示范而不只是嘴上说。好的老师或家长会先演示一步，让孩子自己试，然后给反馈，而不是直接代做。表扬也很重要，但要具体——「这次你握笔比上次好」比笼统的「真棒」更有效。最重要的是大人得有耐心，因为催孩子通常会毁掉他们的兴趣。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":3,"en":"Adults usually learn more deliberately. We read about it, look up tutorials, and often have a specific goal — like learning Excel for work. The advantage is we understand why we're doing it and can ask better questions. The disadvantage is we have less free time and are more embarrassed about making mistakes, so many adults give up in the early stage when progress feels slow.","cn":"成年人一般学得更刻意。我们会去查资料、看教程，而且通常有明确目标——比如为了工作学Excel。优势是我们清楚自己为什么学，也能问出更好的问题。劣势是空闲时间少，而且更怕犯错，所以很多人会在前期进步很慢的阶段就放弃了。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":4,"en":"The biggest difference is feedback. With a teacher, mistakes are corrected quickly, so you avoid building bad habits, and there's someone to keep you motivated. Learning by yourself is more flexible and cheaper, and it forces you to think independently, but it's easy to drift or get stuck. Honestly, the best approach is usually a mix — guided at the start, then self-directed once you have the basics.","cn":"最大的区别是反馈。有老师，错误能被及时纠正，避免养成坏习惯，而且有人能督促你。自学更灵活也更省钱，还能逼你独立思考，但很容易走偏或者卡住。老实说，最好的方式通常是结合——入门时有指导，打好基础后再自主学习。"},{"b":"02","c":3,"i":5,"en":"Because nobody's watching at that stage. The early phase of learning is boring and full of mistakes, and there's no quick reward, so the only thing pushing you forward is your own discipline. Without it, you simply stop. That's why people who achieve a high level in anything usually aren't the most talented — they're the ones who kept going when it wasn't fun anymore.","cn":"因为那个阶段没人盯着你。学东西的初期又枯燥又容易出错，还没有即时回报，所以唯一推着你往前走的就是自律。没有它，你就直接停下来了。这也是为什么任何领域的高手往往不是最有天赋的，而是那些在不再好玩的时候还能坚持下去的人。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":0,"en":"Usually for a mix of reasons. Being funny or good at a sport definitely helps, but I've noticed that the most popular students tend to be the ones who are genuinely easy to be around — they listen, they don't judge, and they include others. Being top of the class can actually work against you if you come across as arrogant. So it's more about social skills than achievements.","cn":"原因通常有几方面。幽默或者运动好确实有帮助，但我注意到最受欢迎的同学往往是相处起来舒服的那种——愿意听别人说、不评判、也愿意带上别人。成绩最好反而可能不利，如果你显得傲慢的话。所以这更多关乎社交能力，而不是成就。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":1,"en":"It helps, but it shouldn't be the goal. A popular teacher has better classroom atmosphere — students pay attention and are more willing to speak up, which really helps with subjects like languages. But if popularity comes from being too easy-going, discipline collapses and nobody learns anything. The best teachers are respected rather than liked, and often they end up being liked precisely because they're fair and clear.","cn":"有帮助，但不该成为目标。受欢迎的老师课堂氛围更好——学生更专注、也更愿意开口，这对语言这类科目帮助很大。但如果受欢迎是靠过度宽松换来的，纪律就会崩掉，谁也学不到东西。最好的老师是被尊重的，而不是被喜欢的；而且往往正因为公正、清晰，最后反而被学生喜欢。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":2,"en":"Not necessarily. Some excellent teachers are quite strict and even a bit intimidating, but students respect them because they explain things clearly and treat everyone fairly. Meanwhile, a teacher can be very friendly but not actually teach much. So popularity and effectiveness aren't the same thing. That said, a teacher who's both respected and approachable is probably ideal, because students feel safe asking questions.","cn":"不一定。有些很优秀的老师相当严格，甚至有点让人怕，但学生尊重他们，因为他们讲得清楚、对所有人公平。反过来，有的老师很亲切，但实际没教多少东西。所以受欢迎和教得好不是一回事。不过既被尊重又好接近的老师大概是最理想的，因为学生会觉得问问题很安全。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":3,"en":"I'd say three things matter most. First, subject knowledge — you can't bluff your way through. Second, the ability to explain difficult ideas in simple language, which is a real skill in itself. And third, patience, because students learn at different speeds and a good teacher doesn't make the slower ones feel stupid. I'd also add fairness, because nothing loses a class's trust faster than obvious favouritism.","cn":"我认为有三点最重要。第一是专业知识，这个糊弄不过去。第二是能把难懂的概念用简单的话讲清楚，这本身就是一种本事。第三是耐心，因为学生进度不同，好老师不会让慢的人觉得自己笨。我还想加上公正，因为没有什么比明显的偏心更快失去全班的信任。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":4,"en":"Yes, in some ways. Social media lets anyone reach a big audience, so you don't need to be a celebrity or an athlete to become well known. But I'd argue it's also harder in a different sense, because the competition is global and you're competing with millions of people doing the same thing. Plus, online popularity is fragile — it can disappear overnight, whereas real popularity built on character lasts much longer.","cn":"在某种程度上是的。社交媒体让任何人都能触达大量受众，所以你不必是明星或运动员就能出名。但我觉得换个角度看其实更难了，因为竞争是全球性的，你在和几百万做同样事情的人竞争。而且网上的热度很脆弱，可能一夜之间就消失；而建立在性格上的真实人缘则持久得多。"},{"b":"02","c":4,"i":5,"en":"For most people it's about belonging. Being popular means people like you and want you around, which satisfies a deep need to feel accepted. Some also want influence — being popular makes it easier to lead or get opportunities. And for a few, it's about validation, like getting likes feels like proof that they matter. The problem is when it becomes an addiction, because then you're living for other people's approval rather than your own.","cn":"对大多数人来说是为了归属感。受欢迎意味着别人喜欢你、愿意和你在一起，这满足了被接纳的深层需求。有些人还想获得影响力——受欢迎更容易做领导或得到机会。还有少数人是为了被认可，比如点赞多了就像证明自己有价值。问题在于如果上瘾了，那就变成活在别人的认可里，而不是为自己活。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":0,"en":"I think there's a balance to strike. Teachers should be aware if a child is in trouble — being bullied, or withdrawing, or clearly unwell — because school is often the only place that notices. But constant monitoring is unhealthy; children need some privacy to learn independence. So I'd say teachers should watch patterns rather than track every single move, and step in when something looks genuinely wrong.","cn":"我觉得要把握一个度。老师应该知道孩子是不是遇到了麻烦——被欺负、变得退缩、或者明显身体不适——因为学校往往是唯一能察觉的地方。但时时刻刻盯着并不健康，孩子需要一点隐私来学会独立。所以我觉得老师应该看模式，而不是追踪每个动作，只在明显不对劲的时候介入。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":1,"en":"To some extent, yes. Teachers see children every day, so they often notice changes that parents miss — a quiet child suddenly becoming aggressive, or someone who stops joining in. But it's not always easy, because unhappy children are often good at hiding it, and teachers have thirty other students to watch. So the real answer is that teachers can spot the signs, but they need training and support to act on them properly.","cn":"一定程度上是的。老师天天见到孩子，所以往往能发现家长忽略的变化——安静的孩子突然变得有攻击性，或者某个人不再参与集体活动。但也不总容易，因为不开心的孩子常常很会掩饰，而老师还要照看另外三十个学生。所以真正的答案是：老师能发现迹象，但需要培训和支持才能正确处理。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":2,"en":"I think art plays a big role. Films, music and stories let people experience emotions safely — you can cry at a sad film and feel lighter afterwards. They also spread hope and connect people, because sharing a song or a show creates a sense of 'we're not alone'. And on the other side, art can challenge negative emotions too, like when a play makes people think about injustice. So overall it has a healing and unifying effect.","cn":"我觉得艺术作用很大。电影、音乐、故事让人能安全地体验情绪——看一部悲伤的电影哭一场，之后反而轻松了。它们也传播希望、连接人心，因为分享一首歌或一部剧会带来「我们不孤单」的感觉。反过来，艺术也能冲击负面情绪，比如一部话剧让人反思不公。所以总体上它有治愈和凝聚的作用。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":3,"en":"I'd say people are happiest in simple, unplanned moments — often when they're with people they love and not thinking about work. For me it's usually a meal with my family, or laughing with friends over something silly. Interestingly, research suggests experiences make us happier than buying things, because memories last longer. And there's something about being fully absorbed in an activity — like cooking or playing a sport — that produces real contentment.","cn":"我觉得人最开心的是那些简单、没计划的时刻——往往是在和爱的人在一起、不去想工作的时候。对我来说通常是和家人吃饭，或者和朋友因为一点傻事笑个不停。有意思的是，研究表明体验比买东西更让人快乐，因为记忆留存更久。而且当你完全投入一件事——比如做饭或运动——会产生一种真正的满足感。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":4,"en":"I think three things. First, feeling competent — students are happiest when they understand the material and see progress. Second, good relationships; having at least one friend or a teacher who respects them matters more than grades. And third, having some choice, like a say in what to study or how to spend break time. What makes students miserable is usually the opposite: confusion, isolation and being controlled all day.","cn":"我想有三点。第一是有能力感——学生在听懂内容、看到进步时最开心。第二是良好的人际关系；至少有一个人朋友，或者一个尊重他的老师，这比成绩更重要。第三是有选择权，比如对学什么、休息时间怎么安排能有点发言权。而让学生痛苦的通常是反面：听不懂、被孤立、整天被管着。"},{"b":"02","c":5,"i":5,"en":"I'd say it's extremely important, maybe more than salary in the long run. If you dislike your job, you spend most of your waking hours stressed, and that affects your health and your relationships too. A job you like also tends to make you better at it, because you actually care. That said, I wouldn't say it's everything — money and security matter, especially if you support a family, so ideally you want both rather than sacrificing one completely.","cn":"我觉得非常重要，长期看可能比薪水还重要。如果你讨厌自己的工作，那你清醒时的大部分时间都在压力中度过，这还会影响健康和你的人际关系。喜欢的工作往往也能让你做得更好，因为你真的在意。不过我也不认为这是全部——钱和稳定也很重要，尤其是要养家的时候，所以理想是两者兼顾，而不是完全牺牲其中一个。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":0,"en":"Yes, definitely. PE isn't really about producing athletes — it's about building habits and health. Children who exercise regularly sleep better, concentrate better and are less stressed, which actually helps their academic work. It also teaches teamwork and how to lose gracefully. And with so many kids now sitting in front of screens all day, school PE might be the only exercise they get, so it's essential.","cn":"当然应该。体育课其实不是为了培养运动员，而是为了养成习惯和保持健康。经常运动的孩子睡得更好、注意力更集中、压力更小，这反而有助于学习。它还能教团队合作和体面地接受失败。而且现在太多孩子整天坐在屏幕前，学校的体育课可能是他们唯一的运动，所以很必要。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":1,"en":"I'd say three things. Physical talent obviously matters, but discipline is what separates good athletes from great ones — showing up to train every day when nobody's watching. Then mental strength, because sport is full of losses and setbacks, and you have to bounce back. And I'd add the ability to work in a team, because even in individual sports there are coaches and support staff around you.","cn":"我觉得有三点。身体天赋当然重要，但真正区分优秀和顶尖运动员的是自律——在没人看着的时候也每天去训练。然后是心理素质，因为运动充满失败和挫折，你得能重新站起来。我还想加上团队合作能力，因为即使是个人项目，你身边也有教练和支持团队。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":2,"en":"Yes, talent matters, especially at the top level — you can't teach height or natural speed. But I'd argue it's not the deciding factor for most people. Hard work can take you much further than raw talent alone, and ironically, very talented people sometimes fail because they never had to work hard as children, so they crumble when they finally meet someone better. The ideal is talent plus work ethic.","cn":"是的，天赋重要，尤其在顶尖水平——身高和天生的速度是教不出来的。但我认为对大多数人来说它不是决定性因素。努力能带你走得比单靠天赋远得多；讽刺的是，非常有天赋的人有时反而失败，因为小时候不用努力，等真遇到更强的对手时就崩了。最理想的是天赋加上努力。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":3,"en":"Honestly, not easy at all. Children often don't show their real strengths until they're older, and a kid who seems average at ten can be exceptional at sixteen, because growth rates differ so much. Also, what looks like talent is sometimes just early practice. So I'd say parents and coaches can spot interest and potential, but declaring someone 'talented' too early can be misleading and even harmful, because it puts pressure on them.","cn":"说实话一点都不容易。孩子往往要到更大一些才显露真正的长处，十岁看起来普通的孩子十六岁可能非常出色，因为发育速度差别很大。而且看起来像天赋的，有时只是早期练习的结果。所以我觉得家长和教练能发现兴趣和潜力，但过早断言谁「有天赋」会误导人，甚至有害，因为会给孩子压力。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":4,"en":"In China I'd say table tennis, badminton and basketball are the most widely played, and football has a huge following even though our national team isn't strong. If we're talking about spectator popularity, table tennis is almost a national sport because we dominate internationally. Running and gym workouts have also exploded in recent years, especially among young office workers who want to stay fit.","cn":"在中国，我觉得乒乓球、羽毛球和篮球参与最广，足球的粉丝也很多，尽管国家队不算强。如果论观赏热度，乒乓球几乎算国球，因为我们在国际上长期占优。跑步和健身近几年也爆火，尤其在想保持健康的年轻上班族中间。"},{"b":"02","c":6,"i":5,"en":"Because the pyramid is incredibly steep. Millions of children start playing a sport, but only a tiny fraction have the combination of talent, years of intense training and luck with injuries. Most drop out when schoolwork takes priority. And even among those who make it professionally, only a handful reach world class. So it's not that talent is rare — it's that reaching the very top requires everything to line up at once.","cn":"因为金字塔太陡了。几百万孩子开始从事一项运动，但只有极少数人能同时具备天赋、多年高强度训练和不受重伤的运气。大多数人会因为学业优先而退出。即便成了职业选手，也只有一小撮人能到世界级水平。所以并不是天赋罕见，而是要登顶需要所有条件同时具备。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":0,"en":"Yes, it's tougher than ever in some ways. Competition is fierce, costs are high, and consumer tastes change quickly, so a business can go from popular to forgotten in a couple of years. On top of that, global uncertainty — like supply problems or economic slowdowns — hits small companies hardest. But the flip side is that technology has lowered entry barriers, so it's also easier than before to start something small online.","cn":"是的，某些方面比以前更难了。竞争激烈、成本高，而且消费者口味变化很快，一家公司可能两三年就从流行变成被遗忘。此外，全球不确定性——比如供应链问题或经济放缓——对中小企业打击最大。但另一面是技术降低了进入门槛，所以现在在网上做点小生意也比以前容易。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":1,"en":"There's a lot they can do. First, cutting waste and emissions in their own operations, like using renewable energy and reducing packaging. Second, designing products that last longer or can be recycled, instead of built-in obsolescence. And third, using their influence — companies can pressure suppliers to be greener and can fund environmental projects. Honestly though, real change only happens when it's also profitable or required by law, so regulation matters.","cn":"他们能做很多。第一，在自身运营中减少浪费和排放，比如用可再生能源、减少包装。第二，设计更耐用或可回收的产品，而不是刻意做短寿命。第三，运用影响力——企业可以施压供应商变得更环保，也能资助环保项目。不过说实话，只有当环保同时有利可图或有法律强制时，真正的改变才会发生，所以监管很重要。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":2,"en":"No, I don't think so. Money is a measure of wealth, not success. Some of the richest people I've read about are miserable, and plenty of people with modest incomes feel genuinely fulfilled. That said, money does buy security and freedom, which makes happiness easier — it's just not the same thing. So I'd say money can contribute to success, but it shouldn't be the definition of it.","cn":"我觉得不算。金钱衡量的是财富，不是成功。我读到过的一些最有钱的人其实很不快乐，而很多收入普通的人却过得很充实。当然，钱确实能买来安全感和自由，让幸福更容易实现——但它并不等于幸福。所以我觉得金钱可以助推成功，但不该是成功的定义。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":3,"en":"I'd say a few things. Reputation matters a lot — a company that treats customers and staff fairly earns trust that pays off long term. Innovation is also key, because standing still means falling behind. And I'd add responsibility: how a company affects the environment and society increasingly influences whether people want to buy from it or work for it. So purpose, not just profit, keeps a company alive.","cn":"我觉得有几点。声誉很重要——公平对待客户和员工的公司能赢得信任，这在长期会带来回报。创新也关键，因为原地不动就等于落后。我还想加上责任：一家公司如何影响环境和社会，越来越影响人们愿不愿意买它的东西、愿不愿意为它工作。所以是使命感而不只是利润，让公司活得长久。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":4,"en":"Starting one is easy on paper — anyone can register a company. But making it survive is very hard. Most new businesses fail within a few years, usually because of cash flow problems, not lack of ideas. Rents, salaries and taxes come before profit, and you need customers fast. So the barrier to entry is low, but the barrier to survival is high, especially in crowded markets.","cn":"形式上创业很容易——谁都能注册一家公司。但要活下来非常难。大多数新公司在几年内就倒闭，通常是因为现金流问题，而不是缺想法。房租、工资和税排在利润前面，而你必须很快找到客户。所以进入门槛低，但生存门槛高，尤其是在竞争饱和的市场里。"},{"b":"02","c":7,"i":5,"en":"I'd say sectors tied to technology, health and green energy are most likely to succeed, because that's where government support and consumer demand are both growing. Electric vehicles and solar are obvious examples. Online services and e-commerce also keep expanding, since people increasingly live through their phones. More traditional sectors like heavy manufacturing face more pressure, though those that upgrade their technology can still do well.","cn":"我觉得和科技、健康、绿色能源相关的行业最容易成功，因为那里既有政策支持，消费需求也在增长。电动车和光伏就是明显的例子。线上服务和电商也在持续扩张，因为人们越来越依赖手机生活。像重工业这种更传统的行业面临更大压力，不过那些完成技术升级的还是能做得不错。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":0,"en":"I'd say being organized saves you a lot of stress. My roommate plans her week every Sunday night, and honestly she never misses a deadline while I'm always rushing. When your life is tidy, you waste less time hunting for things, and you feel more in control. For me it's less about being perfect and more about not panicking.","cn":"我觉得有条理会省下特别多压力。我室友每周日晚上就把下一周安排好，说实话她从来不会误了截止日期，而我总是在赶。生活整齐了，你就不太会浪费时间到处找东西，也更有掌控感。对我来说重点不是做到完美，而是遇到事不慌。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":1,"en":"Yes, I think it's worth starting early. Little kids can learn simple habits, like putting toys back in the box or laying out their clothes the night before. My nephew's school gives the class a tidy-desk star every Friday, and you'd be surprised how much they care. Those small routines stick far better than rules imposed later.","cn":"是的，我觉得早点开始挺值得。小孩子能学些简单的习惯，比如把玩具放回箱子、睡前把衣服摆好。我侄子的学校每周五给全班的整洁小达人之星，你会惊讶他们有多在意。这些小习惯比后来强加的规定扎根得多。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":2,"en":"Probably, to some extent. A student who keeps clear notes and plans revision is less likely to hand work in late, so teachers trust them more. But I wouldn't say it guarantees success. I know a very messy artist who is brilliant at her job because she's so creative. Organization helps, but it isn't the whole story.","cn":"在一定程度上吧。笔记清楚、复习有安排的学生更少迟交作业，所以老师更信任他们。但我不觉得这就保证成功。我认识一个特别乱的艺术家，她工作上却很出色，因为太有创造力了。有条理有帮助，但不是全部。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":3,"en":"A few small tricks work well. Writing a to-do list the night before is a big one, because you wake up knowing exactly where to start. Some people use phone reminders or a calendar. My cousin sorts her emails into folders every morning. The key is finding a system you'll actually stick with, not copying someone else's.","cn":"有几个小办法挺管用。睡前写个待办清单是最有效的，因为你一醒来就知道从哪开始。有人用手机提醒或者日历。我表姐每天早上把邮件归类到文件夹里。关键是找到你真能坚持下去的方法，而不是照搬别人的。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I'd say parents have a big role, mostly by example. If the kitchen is always chaos, telling a child to tidy their room won't work. In my family, my mum keeps a shared calendar on the fridge, and we all check it. Kids copy what they see. So the best lesson is a parent who lives the habit themselves.","cn":"是的，我觉得父母作用很大，主要是靠以身作则。如果厨房总是乱糟糟的，光叫孩子收拾房间是没用的。在我家，我妈在冰箱上放了个共享日历，我们都去看。孩子是照着看到的学的。所以最好的教育就是父母自己先做到。"},{"b":"02","c":8,"i":5,"en":"Definitely. A manager who's disorganized creates chaos for everyone, because the whole team waits on their decisions. If they don't keep track of deadlines, projects slip. I once worked under a boss who never planned meetings, so we kept redoing work. But I'd add that being organized shouldn't mean being rigid; flexibility matters too.","cn":"当然。一个没条理的经理会给所有人制造混乱，因为整支队伍都得等他的决定。如果他不跟踪截止日期，项目就会延误。我曾在一个从不安排会议的老板手下干过，结果我们老返工。但我想补充，有条理不该意味着死板，灵活性也很重要。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":0,"en":"I think people take photos to hold onto moments before they slip away. My friend shoots everything on her phone, from breakfast to sunsets. Part of it is memory, part of it is sharing on social media. For her it's also a hobby that feels creative, like she's telling a story with the photos she keeps.","cn":"我觉得人们拍照是为了在那些瞬间溜走前留住它们。我朋友用手机什么都拍，从早餐到日落。一部分是为了记忆，一部分是为了发社交媒体分享。对她来说这也是一种有创造感的爱好，像在用留下的照片讲故事。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":1,"en":"Honestly, a lot of it is everyday stuff. People snap food, selfies, their pets, and the sky when it looks pretty. When I scroll through my camera roll, it's mostly meals and my cat. On trips, though, it shifts to landscapes and landmarks. So it really depends on whether you're in daily life or somewhere new.","cn":"说实话，很多都是日常的东西。人们拍食物、自拍、宠物，天好看的时候拍天空。我翻自己相册，大多是饭菜和我家猫。不过出门旅游时就变成风景和地标了。所以这真的取决于你是在日常生活还是在陌生的地方。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":2,"en":"It depends where you are. Outdoors or at a nice view, sure, go ahead. But in museums, hospitals or places of worship it can be rude, and some even ban it. Once I saw a tourist photographing people in a temple without asking, and it felt disrespectful. So I'd say take photos, just be aware of the setting and the people around you.","cn":"这要看你在哪儿。户外或者风景好的地方，当然可以随便拍。但在博物馆、医院或者宗教场所，拍照可能不礼貌，有些地方甚至禁止。有次我看见一个游客在寺庙里不打招呼就拍人，让人感觉很不尊重。所以我觉得拍照可以，只是要注意场合和周围的人。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":3,"en":"Mainly because they want to preserve the experience. A photo is proof you were there, and it helps you remember the feeling later. My parents still flick through pictures from a trip we took years ago and instantly recall little details. Sharing them with friends back home is part of it too. It's like bringing the journey back with you.","cn":"主要是因为他们想保存那段经历。照片是你到过那里的证明，还能帮你日后回想起当时的感觉。我父母现在还会翻很多年前一次旅行的照片，一下就想起各种小细节。和家里的朋友分享也是其中一部分。就像把那趟旅程带回来一样。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":4,"en":"The obvious one is that you stop actually looking. People spend the whole trip behind a screen and miss the real thing. My brother once took hundreds of photos at a concert and barely remembered the music. There's also the hassle of always protecting your phone. So I think a balance is best, shoot a few, then put the camera down.","cn":"最明显的一点是你不再真正用眼睛看了。人们整趟旅行都躲在屏幕后面，错过了真实的体验。我哥有一次在演唱会上拍了几百张照片，却几乎没记住音乐。还有就是老得护着手机挺麻烦。所以我觉得平衡最好，拍几张，然后就把相机放下。"},{"b":"02","c":9,"i":5,"en":"Usually at special moments, I'd say. Birthdays, weddings, graduations, the first day of school. People also love golden hour, when the light is soft, so evenings are popular. And when something funny happens, the phone comes out straight away. For me it's whenever a moment feels too good to let go of.","cn":"我觉得通常是在特别的时刻。生日、婚礼、毕业、开学第一天。人们也喜欢黄金时刻，光线柔和，所以傍晚很受欢迎。还有遇到好玩的事，手机马上就拿出来了。对我来说，凡是某个瞬间美好得舍不得放手的，就会想拍。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":0,"en":"Beijing and Shanghai are the obvious ones, and honestly during holidays places like Xi'an and Chengdu get packed too. Last spring I went to Hangzhou over a long weekend and just couldn't move in the old town. It's partly jobs, partly tourism. Big cities pull in young people, so the crowds keep growing.","cn":"北京和上海是最明显的，说实话节假日像西安、成都也挤爆了。去年春天一个长假我去了杭州，在老城区根本走不动。一部分是工作机会，一部分是旅游。大城市吸引年轻人，所以人越来越多。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":1,"en":"I'd say more, but it depends on the city. In places like Shenzhen, planners are adding parks and rooftop gardens because people complained they needed green. But in fast-growing inland cities, building often wins over trees. So I'm hopeful overall, yet I think it'll be uneven across the country.","cn":"我觉得会更多，但要看城市。像深圳这种地方，规划者正在增加公园和屋顶花园，因为人们抱怨需要绿意。但在快速扩张的内陆城市，盖楼常常压过种树。所以总体我挺乐观，但我觉得全国各地会不太均衡。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":2,"en":"More, I hope. Many cities are now building bike lanes and shared-bike systems, and young people like the idea of a healthy, cheap commute. In my neighbourhood, e-bikes are already everywhere. But if public transport doesn't connect well, people will still choose cars. So bike use will rise, just not everywhere at once.","cn":"我希望是更多。很多城市现在在建自行车道和共享单车系统，年轻人喜欢这种健康又便宜的出行。在我住的小区，电动车已经到处都是了。但如果公共交通接得不顺，人们还是会选开车。所以骑车的人会变多，只是不会一下全面铺开。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":3,"en":"Often it's about the atmosphere. Markets, festivals, shopping streets, people go there to feel the energy and to be part of something. My friends and I once queued an hour for a famous hotpot place just because it was the in-thing. It's not logical, but the buzz itself is the fun.","cn":"常常是为了气氛。集市、节日、商业街，人们去那儿是为了感受那种热闹、成为人群的一部分。我和朋友曾排队一小时等一家网红火锅店，就因为它是当下的潮流。这不理性，但那种热闹本身就是乐趣。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":4,"en":"Yes, definitely. Some people are true crowd-lovers; they hate quiet rooms and would rather be surrounded by noise. My uncle is like that, he always chooses the busiest food court. I think it's partly personality, partly habit. So crowded places aren't just something we endure, some of us genuinely seek them out.","cn":"是的，当然。有些人是真的喜欢人多的场合，他们受不了安静的房间，宁愿被嘈杂包围。我叔叔就是这样，总挑最热闹的美食广场。我觉得一部分是性格，一部分是习惯。所以拥挤的地方不只是我们忍受的东西，有些人真的会主动去找。"},{"b":"02","c":10,"i":5,"en":"I'm a bit on the fence. Big cities bring jobs, good hospitals and chances, so a lot of people benefit. But if they're too big, you get terrible traffic and expensive housing, which is what we see in Beijing. So I'd say growth is fine up to a point, but it needs planning, otherwise it drains everyone.","cn":"我有点拿不定主意。大城市带来工作、好的医院和机会，所以很多人受益。但如果太大，就会有糟糕的交通和昂贵的房价，就像我们在北京看到的。所以我觉得增长到一定程度是好的，但需要规划，不然会把每个人都拖垮。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I think cities really need them. Parks give people a place to breathe, walk and exercise, and they cool the streets in summer. In my city there's a small lake park, and on weekends it's full of families. Without spots like that, city life would feel like pure concrete. So they're not a luxury, they're basic.","cn":"是的，我觉得城市真的需要。公园给人们一个呼吸、散步、锻炼的地方，夏天还能给街道降温。我们市里有个小湖公园，周末全是家庭。没有这样的地方，城市生活会像是纯粹的混凝土。所以它们不是奢侈品，是基本需要。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":1,"en":"Definitely. Even a short walk in a park can calm you down. When I've had a stressful week at work, half an hour among trees resets my mood. There's research on this, being in green space lowers stress, and I believe it. So yes, nature is a simple but powerful way to change how you feel.","cn":"当然。就算在公园里散一小会儿步都能让你平静下来。工作压力大的一周之后，在树丛里待半小时就能让我的心情重置。这方面有研究，身处绿地能降低压力，我信。所以是的，大自然是简单但很有力地改变你情绪的方式。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":2,"en":"Honestly, I'd put the environment first. If we wreck the air and water, no amount of money fixes it, and our health pays the price. But I get that poor regions need jobs, so it's a real tension. The best path is green development, like solar and eco-tourism, which can fund protection. So it's not simply pick one.","cn":"说实话，我会把环境放在第一位。如果毁了空气和水，再多的钱也补不回来，而且我们的健康会付出代价。但我明白贫困地区需要工作，所以这是个真实的矛盾。最好的路是绿色发展，比如太阳能和生态旅游，既能赚钱又能支撑保护。所以不是简单二选一。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":3,"en":"I think it's a mix. Some are people who grew up in the countryside and miss it. Others are city workers who need a break from screens and noise. My aunt, a total city person, started hiking last year and now she's addicted. So it's not one type, it's anyone who wants to slow down and feel calmer.","cn":"我觉得是各种人都有。有些是在乡下长大、怀念那里的人。另一些是需要从屏幕和噪音中喘口气的城市上班族。我姑姑本来是纯城市型的人，去年开始徒步，现在上瘾了。所以不是某一类人，而是任何想放慢节奏、想平静下来的人。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I really do. Most of us spend our days indoors staring at phones or computers, and we forget how good fresh air feels. Even a lunchtime walk in a park helps. I'm not saying everyone should move to the mountains, but regular time outdoors keeps us healthier and happier. It should be a habit, not a treat.","cn":"是的，我真心觉得应该。我们大多数人的白天都待在室内盯着手机或电脑，都忘了新鲜空气有多舒服。哪怕是午休在公园走走都有用。我不是说每个人都该搬到山里去，但经常到户外待着会让我们更健康更快乐。这应该成为一种习惯，而不是一种奢侈。"},{"b":"02","c":11,"i":5,"en":"There's a lot they can do. Building more parks and green belts is the obvious one, and keeping them free so everyone can go. Good public transport to reach them matters too. Some cities now run free entry days or organise guided walks. In my view, the government should make nature easy and cheap to reach, not a distant trip.","cn":"他们能做的很多。多建公园和绿化带是最明显的，而且免费开放让所有人都能去。到那里的公共交通方便也很重要。有些城市现在有免费开放日或者组织有向导的徒步。在我看来，政府应该让大自然变得容易且便宜地到达，而不是一趟遥远的旅程。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I'd say they're very valuable. Old buildings hold stories and show how people used to live and build. When I visited a centuries-old temple near my hometown, you could feel the history in the worn steps. They also teach younger generations. So they're worth far more than the land they sit on.","cn":"是的，我觉得它们非常宝贵。老建筑承载着故事，展示过去人们如何生活、如何建造。我去过我家乡附近一座有几百年历史的寺庙，你能在磨损的台阶上感受到历史。它们也能教育年轻一代。所以它们远不止占的那块地值钱。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":1,"en":"No, definitely not all of them. Some are dangerous or have no real value, so knocking them down makes sense. But you can't just demolish everything for new flats. Once it's gone, it's gone forever. So I'd say judge each one, keep the ones with history or beauty, and safely remove the genuinely unsafe ones.","cn":"不，当然不是全部。有些很危险或者没什么真正价值，拆掉是合理的。但你不能为了新公寓就全拆了。一旦没了，就永远没了。所以我觉得要一栋栋地判断，保留那些有历史或有美感的，把确实不安全的拆掉。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":2,"en":"It depends on your situation. Apartments are cheaper, safer and need less upkeep, which suits young workers and older people. But a house gives you a garden and more space, which is great for families with kids. My cousin moved from a flat to a small house just so her kids could play outside. So neither is simply better.","cn":"这要看你的情况。公寓更便宜、更安全、维护也少，适合年轻上班族和老人。但独栋房子有个院子、空间更大，对有孩子的家庭很好。我表姐从公寓搬到一个小房子，就为了让孩子们能在户外玩。所以没有哪个绝对更好。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":3,"en":"Mostly to connect with the past and to see something beautiful. Standing in a place where real events happened feels different from reading about it in a book. People also go for the architecture or the view. On my last trip I spent an hour just looking at the carved wooden ceiling; it was stunning.","cn":"主要是为了和过去建立联系、看些美的东西。站在真发生过事情的地方，和从书里读到是不同的。人们也会为了建筑本身或风景去。上次旅行我花了一个小时就看那雕花的木天花板，太美了。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":4,"en":"I wouldn't say replace, more like coexist. New buildings are needed for homes and offices, but wiping out the old would make cities look identical everywhere. In some Chinese cities you now see glass towers right next to ancient temples, and honestly that mix is what makes them interesting. So I'd keep both rather than choose.","cn":"我不会说替代，更像是共存。新建筑对住房和办公是必要的，但抹掉旧的会让所有城市长得一模一样。在中国一些城市，你现在能看到玻璃高楼紧挨着古庙，说实话正是这种混搭让它们有意思。所以我宁愿两者都留，而不是二选一。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":5,"en":"Yes, hugely important. They're links to our history and identity, and once demolished they can't come back. They also attract tourists, which helps the local economy. When I see a restored old street, it feels like the city remembers who it is. So protecting them is about keeping our roots, not just nice photos.","cn":"是的，极其重要。它们连接着我们的历史与身份，一旦拆掉就回不来了。它们也能吸引游客，帮到当地经济。当我看到修复好的老街，感觉这座城市记得自己是谁。所以保护它们是在守住我们的根，而不只是拍好看的照片。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":6,"en":"Several reasons, really. Restoration is expensive and needs skilled craftsmen who are hard to find. Owners may want to sell the land for profit, and new development often wins. Plus, weather and pollution slowly damage the materials. My local old town nearly lost a wooden theatre just because nobody could fund the repairs.","cn":"说实话有几个原因。修复很贵，还需要手艺难找的匠人。业主可能想把地卖了赚钱，而新开发常常占上风。再加上天气和污染慢慢侵蚀材料。我们当地的老城就差点失去一座木戏楼，就因为没有钱修。"},{"b":"02","c":12,"i":7,"en":"I think it's the pace of life. In the countryside things are slower and quieter, and the air is cleaner. Some also go back to be near family or because housing is cheaper. My grandparents' neighbour moved out of the city after retiring and says she finally sleeps well. So for many it's about peace, not just money.","cn":"我觉得是生活节奏。在乡下事情更慢、更安静，空气也更干净。有些人回去是想离家人近些，或者因为住房更便宜。我爷爷奶奶的邻居退休后搬出了城市，她说终于能睡好觉了。所以对很多人来说是为了安宁，不只是钱。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":0,"en":"To some degree, yes. Kids are loud when they're playing and laughing, and that's actually healthy; it means they're active and happy. My little cousins turn the living room into a race track every weekend, and we just laugh. But there's a line between happy noise and screaming in a restaurant. So it's fine, as long as it doesn't disturb others.","cn":"在一定程度上是的。孩子玩起来、笑闹的时候确实吵，但这其实是健康的，说明他们活泼快乐。我小表弟表妹每个周末都把客厅变成赛道，我们就一笑了之。但开心的吵闹和在餐厅里尖叫之间有条线。所以是可以的，只要不打扰别人。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":1,"en":"No, I wouldn't go that far. Children need to express themselves and burn off energy, and a bit of noise is normal. What matters is the place and time. I wouldn't tell a kid to be silent at the playground, but I would expect quiet in a library. So the rule should be about being considerate, not banning noise completely.","cn":"不，我不会那么绝对。孩子需要表达自己、释放精力，有点噪音是正常的。重要的是场合和时间。在游乐场我不会让孩子闭嘴，但在图书馆我会要求安静。所以规矩应该是体贴别人，而不是完全禁止吵闹。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":2,"en":"Oh, all sorts. There's traffic, motorbikes and car horns, which is the big city soundtrack. Then at home you've got washing machines, TV, and my neighbour's renovation going on for months. There are also natural ones, like rain and thunder, which are actually pleasant. And of course people talking, which is the noisiest of all.","cn":"哦，各种各样。有交通、摩托车和汽车喇叭，这是大城市的背景音。在家里有洗衣机、电视，还有我邻居搞了好几个月的装修。也有自然的声音，比如雨声和雷声，其实挺舒服的。当然还有人说话的声音，这才是最吵的。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":3,"en":"Clearly the city. In the countryside you mainly hear birds and wind; at night it's almost silent. In the city it never really stops, there's traffic day and night, alarms, construction. When I visit my grandma in the village, the quiet actually keeps me awake at first. So yes, city dwellers are exposed to far more noise.","cn":"显然是城市。在乡下你主要听到鸟叫和风声，晚上几乎一片寂静。在城市里基本停不下来，日夜都有交通、警报、施工。我去乡下看我奶奶的时候，那份安静一开始反而让我睡不着。所以是的，城市居民接触到的噪音多得多。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":4,"en":"Mostly people just put up with it, I think. Some wear earphones or close the windows. In my building we've complained to the management about late-night noise a few times, and it usually stops. Younger people tend to tolerate it more, while older neighbours will actually go and knock on the door. So the reaction depends on the person.","cn":"我觉得大多数人就是忍着。有的人戴耳机或者关窗。在我们楼里，我们向物业投诉过几次深夜噪音，一般就会停。年轻人比较能忍受，老邻居则会直接去敲门。所以反应因人而异。"},{"b":"02","c":13,"i":5,"en":"Simple things help a lot. Keep your voice down, especially on the phone, and don't play loud videos on speaker. If you're in a quiet space like a train, use earphones. And if you must take a call, step aside. It's really just about remembering there are other people around, and being a bit thoughtful goes a long way.","cn":"简单的做法就很管用。把音量放低，尤其打电话时，别外放视频。如果在火车这种安静的地方，就戴耳机。如果非要接电话，就走开一点。其实就是记住周围还有别人，多替人想一点就很起作用。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":0,"en":"Usually for a reason, I'd say. Birthdays, weddings, graduations and festivals are the big ones. But people also throw parties just to catch up, like a reunion with old classmates. In my family we always gather for the Spring Festival Eve dinner, and it's the loudest, happiest night of the year. So there's always an excuse if you look for one.","cn":"我觉得通常都有个由头。生日、婚礼、毕业和节日是大头。但人们也会只为聚一聚就办派对，比如老同学聚会。在我家，我们每年除夕都会聚在一起吃年夜饭，那是一年中最热闹、最开心的晚上。所以只要你想，总能找到理由。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":1,"en":"I'd say it depends on the occasion. For a small birthday, a private place like home feels warmer and you can relax. For a big celebration, people often hire a public venue or a hall, because there's more space and no cleaning up. My friends rented a cafe for a party once, and it worked perfectly. So both have their moment.","cn":"我觉得要看场合。小型生日在像家这样的私人地方感觉更温馨，也更放松。大型庆祝人们常常租公共场所或者宴会厅，因为空间更大，也不用收拾。我朋友有一次租了家咖啡馆办派对，效果特别好。所以两种都有各自适用的时候。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":2,"en":"Honestly I think more people choose a restaurant. You get food, service and space in one go, and nobody has to cook or wash dishes. A pub is more for casual drinks with close friends, a smaller group. For a family celebration like a grandma's birthday, a restaurant is far more practical. So it really depends on who and what you're celebrating.","cn":"说实话我觉得更多人会选餐厅。吃的、服务、场地一次搞定，谁都不用做饭洗碗。酒吧更适合和好朋友随意喝两杯，人少一点。像奶奶生日这种家庭庆祝，餐厅实用得多。所以真的取决于你和谁、庆祝什么。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":3,"en":"Quite a few. The Spring Festival is the biggest, then Mid-Autumn Festival, when families eat mooncakes and watch the full moon. National Day is huge too, with fireworks and travel. There are also personal ones like weddings and graduations. My family never misses the Lantern Festival, where we hang red lanterns. So there's always something to celebrate.","cn":"挺多的。春节最大，然后是中秋节，一家人吃月饼、赏满月。国庆节也很盛大，有烟花和出行。也有个人的，比如婚礼和毕业。我家从来不错过元宵节，我们会挂红灯笼。所以总有可庆祝的事。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":4,"en":"I don't think it's a waste, but it has limits. National celebrations can bring people together and boost pride, and the parades and fireworks are genuinely moving. But spending millions on a single show when hospitals are short of money does feel wrong. So I'd say celebrate, but keep it sensible and share the budget with real needs.","cn":"我不觉得是浪费，但有个度。国家庆典能把人聚在一起、提升自豪感，阅兵和烟花确实很打动人。但在医院缺钱的时候为一场演出花几百万，感觉就不对了。所以我觉得可以庆祝，但要有分寸，把预算分一些给真正的需要。"},{"b":"02","c":14,"i":5,"en":"I'd say the Spring Festival is the most important by far. It's when families travel huge distances to be together, eat a big dinner and hand out red envelopes. Mid-Autumn Festival is a close second, since it's all about reunion. Those two carry the strongest feelings. Others are fun, but these two are really about family.","cn":"我觉得春节到目前为止最重要。一家人会跑很远的路聚到一起，吃顿大餐、发红包。中秋节紧随其后，因为它讲的就是团圆。这两个节日承载的情感最强。其他节日也好玩，但这两个才是真正关于家人的。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":0,"en":"Honestly, self-help and success books sell really well, like how-to books on getting rich or staying focused. Novels are popular too, especially online ones you read on your phone. My sister reads business books, while I stick to fiction. I'd say the biggest sellers are anything that promises to make your life better or helps you escape for a while.","cn":"说实话，自我提升和成功学类的书卖得特别好，比如教你怎么赚钱或者保持专注的方法书。小说也很火，尤其在手机上读的网络小说。我姐姐读商业书，我则只看小说。我觉得最畅销的是那些承诺能改善你生活、或者帮你暂时逃离现实的书。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":1,"en":"I think reading the book first is usually better, but not always. Books give you more detail and let you build the world in your own head. When I read a novel before the film, I always understand the characters better. But if the book is really long or heavy, the movie can be a good way in. So it depends on your goal.","cn":"我觉得通常先读书更好，但不总是。书给你更多细节，让你在脑子里自己搭建那个世界。当我在电影之前读了小说，我总能更好地理解人物。但如果书太长或者太沉闷，电影倒是个不错的入门方式。所以要看你的目的。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":2,"en":"Not really, to be honest. They can spark ideas for some people, and a good one might give you a push. But I've read a few and they often repeat the same advice, like work hard and stay positive. My friend bought five of them and nothing changed. So they can help a bit, but they're no magic fix for everyone.","cn":"说实话，不太有用。对某些人它们能激发想法，一本好的也许会推你一把。但我读过几本，它们常常重复同样的建议，比如努力、保持积极。我朋友买了五本，什么也没变。所以它们能帮上一点，但对每个人来说都不是灵丹妙药。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":3,"en":"Yes, I think so, sadly. People are busier and spend their free time on short videos and games, which are easier than a book. When I take the subway, almost everyone is staring at a phone, not reading. But audiobooks are helping some people come back. So overall reading has dropped, though it's not dead yet.","cn":"是的，我觉得是这样，挺可惜的。人们更忙了，空闲时间都花在短视频和游戏上，这些比读书更容易。我坐地铁时，几乎人人都在盯手机，不是看书。但有声书在帮一些人回归阅读。所以总体阅读量是下降了，但还没消亡。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":4,"en":"Mainly because it's quicker and easier. A movie tells you the whole story in two hours with sound and pictures, so it needs little effort. Books take patience and imagination, and after a long day, some people just want to switch off. My brother says films help him relax, whereas reading feels like homework. So it's often about energy, not taste.","cn":"主要是因为更快更容易。一部电影两个小时用声音和画面把故事讲完，不需要什么力气。书需要耐心和想象力，一天劳累之后，有些人只想放空。我哥说电影让他放松，而读书感觉像做作业。所以常常是精力问题，而不是品味问题。"},{"b":"02","c":15,"i":5,"en":"A good way is to read together and let the child choose. If parents force classic novels, kids will hate it. My mum used to read picture books with me every night, and I never felt pushed. Bedtime stories are great for this. Also, having books around the house helps. So the trick is making reading feel like fun, not a chore.","cn":"一个好办法是一起读，并让孩子自己挑。如果父母硬塞名著，孩子会反感。我妈以前每晚和我一起读绘本，我从没觉得被逼。睡前故事特别适合。另外，家里有书也有帮助。所以诀窍是让阅读感觉像乐趣，而不是差事。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":0,"en":"I don't think they need to be overly kind, just genuine. What matters is the intention, not how sweet you sound. Helping a friend move house is kindness, even if you're grumpy about it. But being kind shouldn't mean being a pushover either. So I'd say help willingly and respect the person, that's kind enough.","cn":"我不觉得他们需要格外温柔，真诚就行。重要的是心意，而不是你说话多甜。帮朋友搬家就是善意，哪怕你嘴上不情愿。但善良也不该意味着当老好人被人使唤。所以我觉得乐意去帮、尊重对方，这就够善良了。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":1,"en":"Yes, definitely, and early is best. Kids learn kindness by watching, so if they see you help a neighbour carry bags, they'll copy it. My nephew now shares his snacks without being asked, and it's just from what he sees at home. So teaching kindness shouldn't be a lecture; it should be daily practice.","cn":"是的，当然，越早越好。孩子是靠观察学会善良的，如果他们看到你帮邻居拎袋子，他们就会照着做。我侄子现在会主动分零食，就是照着家里看到的学的。所以教善良不该是讲大道理，而应该是每天实践。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":2,"en":"It depends on the age and the subject. For little kids, helping a bit is fine, like checking their spelling. But once they're older, parents shouldn't do the work for them. I know a mum who actually wrote her son's essay, and he learned nothing. So support and explain, yes, but don't take over; that only weakens them.","cn":"这要看年龄和科目。对小孩子，帮一点没问题，比如检查拼写。但等他们大一些，父母就不该替他们做了。我知道一个妈妈真的替儿子写了作文，结果他什么都没学到。所以可以支持和讲解，但别包办，那只会让他们更弱。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":3,"en":"I'd say honest and practical advice, based on what they've seen. Parents have lived through things, so they can warn kids about mistakes. But advice should be a suggestion, not an order. My dad once told me to save money early, and it turned out to be true. So the best advice is honest, useful, and given without pressure.","cn":"我觉得是诚实又实用的建议，基于他们见过的事。父母经历过很多，所以能提醒孩子别犯的错。但建议应该是提议，不是命令。我爸曾让我早点存钱，事实证明他说得对。所以最好的建议是诚实的、有用的，而且不带压力。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":4,"en":"Yes, definitely, because parents have experience kids simply don't have yet. But they should give advice, not commands, or kids will just rebel. When I was choosing my major, my parents suggested options but let me decide, and I respected that. So advice is valuable, as long as it comes with respect and some freedom.","cn":"是的，当然，因为父母有孩子还没有的经验。但他们应该给建议，而不是下命令，不然孩子只会逆反。我选专业时，父母提了选项但让我自己定，我很尊重这一点。所以建议是有价值的，只要它伴随着尊重和一些自由。"},{"b":"02","c":16,"i":5,"en":"Absolutely, in lots of ways. Kids can help with small chores, like setting the table or walking the dog, which eases the load. They can also cheer parents up after a hard day, which sounds small but matters. My little cousin always tells his mum she's the best cook, and it clearly lifts her. So help isn't only physical.","cn":"当然，有很多方式。孩子能帮忙做点小家务，比如摆碗筷、遛狗，这能减轻负担。他们也能在父母劳累一天后逗他们开心，听起来小，但很重要。我小表弟总跟他妈说她是全世界最好的厨师，明显让妈妈很高兴。所以帮忙不只是体力上的。"},{"b":"02","c":17,"i":0,"en":"Honestly, it can be tricky. With so many people there are more opinions and more small arguments, especially between grandparents and teenagers. But bigger families also help each other more. In my aunt's family, five people live together and they cook and babysit as a team. So it takes patience, but the support is worth it.","cn":"说实话，这挺不容易的。人多了意见就多，小摩擦也更多，尤其长辈和青少年之间。但大家庭之间互相帮衬也更多。我姑姑家五口人住一起，他们做饭、带孩子都像一支队伍。所以需要耐心，但那份支持是值得的。"},{"b":"02","c":17,"i":1,"en":"Simple things help, I think. Eating dinner together without phones is a big one, because that's when real conversations happen. Regular family trips or a weekly game night also work. In my family we have a rule that Sunday lunch is phone-free, and it's honestly the best hour of the week. So it's about creating shared time.","cn":"我觉得简单的做法就管用。一起吃晚饭时不看手机是很重要的一点，因为真正的聊天就发生在那个时候。定期的家庭出游或每周的游戏之夜也有用。我家有个规矩：周日午饭不碰手机，说实话那是一周里最好的一小时。所以关键是要创造共处的时间。"},{"b":"02","c":17,"i":2,"en":"I'd say it changes with age. Teenagers usually prefer friends, because that's when you're figuring out who you are and friends feel like your world. But as people get older, many turn back to family. My cousin barely came home at eighteen, but now at thirty she calls her mum every day. So it shifts over time.","cn":"我觉得这随年龄变化。青少年通常更喜欢朋友，因为那时候你正在弄清楚自己是谁，朋友就像你的全世界。但随着年龄增长，很多人又会转向家人。我表姐十八岁时几乎不着家，现在三十岁了，天天给她妈打电话。所以这是会随时间变化的。"},{"b":"02","c":17,"i":3,"en":"Honestly, I think many older people lean towards family, since friends may have moved away or passed on. Family becomes the steady anchor, especially grandchildren. But it's not absolute; my grandpa plays chess with his old friends every morning and clearly needs them too. So for them it's often family and a few close friends together.","cn":"说实话，我觉得很多老人更偏向家人，因为朋友可能搬走了或者过世了。家人成了稳定的依靠，尤其是孙辈。但也不绝对；我爷爷每天早上和老朋友下棋，显然也离不开他们。所以对他们来说，常常是家人和几个密友兼而有之。"},{"b":"02","c":17,"i":4,"en":"Because they give us support we can't get elsewhere. A good friend listens without judging and stands by you when things go wrong. I once had a tough time with work, and a friend just took me out for noodles and let me talk. That night helped more than any advice. So we need friends to feel less alone.","cn":"因为他们给我们别处得不到的支持。好朋友不评判地倾听，在你出事时站在你这边。我曾有过一段工作上的难熬时期，一个朋友就带我出去吃面、让我倾诉。那一晚比任何建议都有用。所以我们需要朋友，是为了不那么孤单。"},{"b":"02","c":18,"i":0,"en":"Definitely, I'd say money management should be part of the curriculum. When I was in primary school, we had a little shop project where we earned fake money for good behaviour and could 'buy' small prizes. Just that simple game taught me the idea of saving up instead of spending everything at once. Real life skills like that stay with you far longer than maths formulas.","cn":"我觉得理财绝对应该放进课程里。我上小学的时候，班里搞过一个'小商店'活动，表现好就能挣到假币，然后去'买'小奖品。就这么个简单的游戏，让我懂得了攒钱，而不是一下子全花光。这种真实生活里的技能，比数学公式记得久多了。"},{"b":"02","c":18,"i":1,"en":"To be honest, I think it should be a mix of both. My parents let me spend my lucky money however I liked, but they also opened a savings account for me and showed me how the interest grew. So I learned to enjoy spending a bit while still putting something aside. Only teaching saving can make kids too tight, and only spending makes them careless.","cn":"说实话，我觉得应该两者结合。我爸妈让我随便花自己的压岁钱，但同时也给我开了个储蓄账户，让我看看利息怎么涨。所以我学会了既享受花钱，又留一点存起来。只教存钱会让孩子太抠，只教花钱又让他们大手大脚。"},{"b":"02","c":18,"i":2,"en":"Spending is definitely much easier, no question. Whenever I go out with friends, money seems to vanish on bubble tea, snacks and little things I don't even remember buying. Saving, on the other hand, takes real self-control, because you're basically saying no to yourself today for something you want later. That's why so many people struggle to put money aside.","cn":"花钱肯定容易多了，这没得说。每次和朋友出去，钱就在奶茶、零食和一些我都不记得买了啥的小东西上没了。存钱就不一样了，真得靠自律，因为你其实是在对今天的自己说不，为了以后想要的东西。所以很多人才存不下钱。"},{"b":"02","c":18,"i":3,"en":"People save for all sorts of things, but I'd say the big ones are housing, education and emergencies. My cousin saved for nearly three years just to put down a deposit on a flat in the city. Others save for a wedding, a car, or a trip abroad. Honestly, having some savings also just gives you peace of mind, in case you lose your job.","cn":"人们存钱的原因五花八门，但我觉得主要就是买房、教育和应急。我表哥存了快三年钱，就为了在城里付个房子的首付。还有人为结婚、买车或者出国旅游存钱。说实话，有点存款也让人心里踏实，万一失业了也不慌。"},{"b":"02","c":18,"i":4,"en":"I'd say both matter, but in different ways. Money obviously helps charities run food banks and shelters, so donating cash is really useful. But my mum volunteers at a community centre every weekend, and honestly her time means more to the old people there than a cheque ever could. So ideally you give whatever you can, money or hours.","cn":"我觉得两者都重要，只是方式不同。钱当然能帮慈善机构运转食物银行和收容所，所以捐钱确实有用。但我妈每个周末都在社区中心做志愿者，说实话，对那里的老人来说，她的时间比一张支票有意义多了。所以理想情况下，你能出什么就出什么，钱或者时间都行。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":0,"en":"I'd say the trick is drawing clear lines. I try to finish work by six, then the evening belongs to me, whether that's cooking or the gym. When I was younger I'd keep checking emails at midnight, and it just wrecked my sleep. Now I switch my phone to silent after dinner. It's not perfect, but that small boundary makes a huge difference to how I feel.","cn":"我觉得诀窍就是划清界限。我尽量六点前把活儿干完，之后晚上就是我自己的时间，做饭也好去健身也好。年轻时我半夜还在查邮件，结果把睡眠搞得一团糟。现在我一吃完晚饭就把手机调成静音。虽然不算完美，但这条小界线对我的状态影响特别大。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":1,"en":"Honestly, not for long. If I realise I'm scrolling through short videos for an hour, I'll stop pretty quickly, because that guilt just isn't worth it. But there's one exception — sometimes I keep chatting with an old friend late at night even though I know I should sleep, because the connection feels more valuable than the lost hour. So it depends on why I'm doing it.","cn":"说实话，撑不了多久。要是我发现自己刷短视频刷了一个小时，我很快就不刷了，因为那种负罪感太不值了。但有个例外——有时候我明知该睡了还跟老朋友聊到大半夜，因为那份感情比丢掉一小时更值。所以得看我是为什么在做这件事。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":2,"en":"Lots of things, really. Deadlines at work are the obvious one — my brother works at a company where every project has a tight schedule, and he's basically always on edge. Money worries pressure people too, especially rent and loans. And to be honest, social media adds pressure these days, because you're constantly comparing your life to everyone else's highlight reel.","cn":"很多事都会让人有压力。工作上的截止日期最明显——我哥那家公司每个项目都排得很紧，他基本一直绷着。钱的烦恼也压人，尤其是房租和贷款。而且说实话，现在社交媒体也在加码，因为你总拿自己的生活和别人晒出来的高光时刻比。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":3,"en":"That's a good one. I think some people break rules simply because they feel the rules are unfair or pointless, so they see no reason to follow them. My little cousin used to ignore the school dress code because he thought it was silly. Others just want to feel free or rebel against authority. But I'd say most of us follow rules as long as they seem reasonable.","cn":"这个问题挺好。我觉得有些人不守规矩，纯粹是因为他们觉得规矩不公平或者没意义，所以觉得没必要遵守。我表弟以前就不理学校的着装规定，因为他觉得那规定很蠢。还有人就是想图个自由，或者跟权威对着干。不过我觉得，只要规矩看着合理，大多数人还是愿意遵守的。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I think a bit of stress is actually healthy. It pushes you to focus and get things done — I always work best the night before a deadline. The problem is when it becomes constant. My dad had a period of nonstop stress at work and ended up with serious stomach trouble. So short bursts are fine, but long-term pressure really damages your health.","cn":"是的，我觉得有点压力其实挺健康的。它会推着你集中精力把事办完——我总是在截止日期的前一晚效率最高。问题在于它变成长期的了。我爸有段时间工作压力没停过，最后把胃搞出了毛病。所以短期压力没问题，但长期紧绷真的会伤身体。"},{"b":"02","c":19,"i":5,"en":"On the whole, yes, I'd say a routine is good. Getting up at the same time and having a rough plan for the day keeps me from wasting hours deciding what to do next. But I also think it shouldn't be too rigid. On weekends I deliberately leave things loose, just go with my mood. A bit of structure plus some freedom is probably the sweet spot.","cn":"总的来说，我觉得有作息规律是好事。每天差不多同一时间起床，再有个大致的日程，能让我不至于花好几个小时纠结接下来干啥。但我也觉得不能太死板。周末我会故意留得松一点，跟着感觉走。有点规律加一点自由，大概就是最好的状态。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":0,"en":"Yes, travelling abroad has become really common for Chinese people. My parents' generation would save for years just to see one country, but now my colleagues pop over to Thailand or Japan for a long weekend without much planning. Flights are cheaper, visas are easier, and social media makes foreign places feel close. So I'd say the appetite for it has grown a lot.","cn":"是的，出国旅游对中国人来说现在太普遍了。我父母那一辈得攒好几年才敢去一个国家看看，而我同事现在去泰国或日本过个长周末，几乎不用怎么计划。机票更便宜了，签证更容易，社交媒体又让国外看着很近。所以我觉得大家出国的热情涨了很多。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":1,"en":"I'd say young people are keener on it overall. My little sister and her friends love chasing cheap flights and posting about it, and they're happy with hostels and street food. Older people often prefer guided tours or visiting relatives, and some just find the whole thing tiring. That said, plenty of retired people now travel too, especially once they have both money and free time.","cn":"总体我觉得年轻人更热衷。我妹妹和她朋友就爱抢廉价机票、到处打卡，住青旅、吃街边小吃都无所谓。上了年纪的人往往更喜欢跟团游或者探亲，有的人干脆觉得折腾。不过话说回来，现在不少退休的人也出门旅游，尤其是既有钱又有闲以后。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":2,"en":"It really depends on the destination, but I'd say one to two weeks is reasonable. Anything shorter and you spend half the time in airports, and you don't really soak in the place. But more than three weeks can get exhausting and expensive. When my aunt went to Europe, two weeks felt about right — long enough to see several cities without feeling rushed.","cn":"这得看目的地，不过我觉得一两周比较合理。再短的话，一半时间都耗在机场上，根本没法好好感受一个地方。但超过三周又会又累又贵。我姑妈去欧洲那次，两周就挺合适——足够看好几个城市，又不至于赶得太紧。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":3,"en":"In my view, yes, it's usually worth the money and time. You come back with a wider view of the world — you realise how differently people live, and that really humbles you. My first trip abroad made me far more open-minded about food and customs. Of course, if you go into debt or rush around ticking off landmarks, then the cost probably outweighs the benefits.","cn":"在我看来，通常还是值这些钱和时间的。回来之后你的眼界会变宽——你会意识到别人的生活方式有多不同，这真的很让人谦卑。我第一次出国就让我对食物和习俗开放了很多。当然，如果你借钱去，或者一路赶着打卡景点，那花的代价可能就大于收获了。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":4,"en":"There are quite a few benefits. You get exposed to a new language and culture, which broadens your thinking. You also pick up practical skills like navigating an unfamiliar city or handling problems on your own. And honestly, the memories and friendships from travelling tend to last a lifetime. My best conversations ever happened with strangers I met in a hostel abroad.","cn":"好处挺多的。你能接触到新的语言和文化，思路会被打开。你还能学到一些实用技能，比如在陌生城市找路、自己处理突发状况。而且说实话，旅途中留下的回忆和交到的朋友往往能记一辈子。我聊得最尽兴的几次，都是和在外地旅舍遇到的陌生人。"},{"b":"02","c":20,"i":5,"en":"There's no fixed answer, but I'd say around two weeks suits most people. Under a week feels rushed, especially if you cross several time zones, because jet lag eats into your first few days. Over a month, though, many people start to miss home and their routine. So for a normal holiday, one to two weeks is enough time, hitting the sweet spot between seeing plenty and not wearing yourself out.","cn":"没有固定答案，不过我觉得两周左右对大多数人合适。不到一周会觉得赶，尤其是跨好几个时区的时候，倒时差就吃掉头几天了。但超过一个月，很多人又会开始想家、想念日常。所以普通度假的话，一到两周的时间就够，既能看够，又不至于把自己累垮。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":0,"en":"I think it's mainly the convenience and the experience. Malls have everything under one roof — clothes, food, a cinema, even a kids' play area — so a family can spend a whole afternoon there without stepping outside. When it's freezing or raining, that's a big deal. They're also air-conditioned and clean, which makes shopping feel more comfortable than a crowded street market.","cn":"我觉得主要是方便和体验。商场里啥都有——服装、吃饭、电影院，甚至有儿童游乐区，所以一家人能待一下午都不用出门。天冷或者下雨的时候，这点特别重要。而且商场有空调、又干净，逛街比拥挤的街边市场舒服多了。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":1,"en":"On the upside, small shops are often cheaper and the owners know you personally, so you get advice you actually trust. My dad buys his shoes from this tiny shop nearby, and the owner always tells him honestly which ones last. The downside is limited choice and no real return policy. If something goes wrong, you can't complain to a big brand behind it.","cn":"好处是，小店通常更便宜，老板也认识你本人，给的建议你信得过。我爸就在附近一家小店买鞋，老板总会老实告诉他哪双耐穿。坏处是选择少，而且基本没有退货一说。要是出了问题，你也没个大品牌可以投诉。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":2,"en":"I'd say it's mostly about trust and choice. In a small shop, you can't always be sure the goods are genuine, and there might be no receipt or warranty, so if it breaks you're stuck. Prices can also be less transparent — some owners charge tourists more. And personally, I find the cramped space a bit stressful, whereas a mall lets you wander at your own pace.","cn":"我觉得主要是信任和选择的问题。在小店里，你不一定搞得清东西是不是正品，可能也没发票和保修，坏了就只能自认倒霉。价格也往往不那么透明——有的老板会宰游客。而且就我个人来说，挤挤巴巴的空间让我有点压抑，而在商场里你可以自己慢慢逛。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":3,"en":"They're pretty different. Online shopping wins on price and range — you can compare hundreds of options and read reviews in minutes, and it's delivered to your door. In-store, though, you can actually touch the product, try it on, and take it home the same day. I buy clothes online but always get fresh food in person, because I want to see it first.","cn":"差别挺大的。网购胜在价格和种类——几分钟就能比较几百个选项、看评论，还送到家门口。但实体店能让你真正摸到商品、试穿，当天就拿走。衣服我会网购，但生鲜我一定去实体店买，因为我想先看看到手的东西。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":4,"en":"Online shopping is super convenient and usually cheaper, and you can shop at midnight in your pyjamas. But the big downside is that what you see isn't always what you get — my friend ordered a coat that looked navy online but turned out bright blue. Returns can be a hassle too, and you miss the fun of browsing and chatting with shop assistants.","cn":"网购超级方便，通常也更便宜，还能穿着睡衣半夜下单。但最大的问题是看到的不一定是实物——我朋友买了件外套，网上看着是藏青色，结果到手是亮蓝色。退货也麻烦，而且你少了逛街和跟店员聊天的乐趣。"},{"b":"02","c":21,"i":5,"en":"Yes, definitely. When people spend, businesses earn, so they hire more staff and invest more, and that drives the whole economy forward. Consumer spending is a huge chunk of GDP in most countries. My hometown is a good example — a new shopping district opened and suddenly cafes and small businesses popped up all around it. Of course, spending has to be sustainable, not driven by debt.","cn":"是的，绝对是。人们花钱，企业就有收入，就会多招人、多投资，从而拉动整个经济。消费支出在大多数国家都是GDP的一大部分。我老家就是个好例子——新开了一个商业区，周围一下就冒出很多咖啡店和小商户。当然，消费得是可持续的，不能靠借钱来撑。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":0,"en":"I'd say it comes down to the consequences. An easy decision, like what to have for lunch, doesn't really change much either way. An important one, like which university to attend or whether to move cities, shapes your life for years. So a simple test is asking yourself: if I get this wrong, how much will it hurt? My choice to study abroad passed that test, which is why it felt so heavy.","cn":"我觉得关键在于后果。简单决定，比如午饭吃啥，怎么选都无所谓。重要决定，比如上哪所大学、要不要搬去另一个城市，会影响你好多年。所以有个简单的检验方法：问自己一句，要是选错了，会有多痛？我当初决定出国留学就通过了这个检验，所以才觉得特别沉重。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":1,"en":"Yes, I think that's actually a healthy habit. Making small choices daily, like what to wear or where to eat, keeps your decision-making muscles in shape, so bigger ones don't freeze you. There's even research that too many choices make us tired. But I'd say it's fine as long as you don't agonise over tiny things — the point is practice, not perfection.","cn":"是的，我觉得这其实是个好习惯。每天做点小选择，比如穿什么、去哪儿吃，能让你的决断力保持在线，遇到大事时不至于卡壳。甚至有研究说，选择太多会让人疲惫。不过我觉得，只要你别在小事上纠结半天就行——重点在于练手，不是追求完美。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":2,"en":"Oh, loads of tiny ones. Things like what to eat for breakfast, whether to take the bus or the subway, what to watch tonight, or whether to reply to a message right away. My mornings are full of these little calls before I've even left the house. None of them matter much alone, but together they shape how your day goes and how much time you waste.","cn":"哦，一大堆小事。比如早饭吃啥、坐公交还是地铁、今晚看什么、消息要不要马上回。我一个早上还没出门就做了一堆这种小决定。单看每一个都不重要，但加在一起，就决定了你这一天过得怎么样、浪费了多少时间。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":3,"en":"That happens all the time, and I think the key is talking it out rather than voting straight away. When my group project had a clash about our topic, we each explained why we preferred ours, and it turned out two of us cared much more than the others. So we went with their idea and the rest of us compromised on the details. Listening first usually beats arguing.","cn":"这种事经常发生，我觉得关键在于先沟通，而不是一上来就投票。我们小组项目选主题时起了分歧，大家各自讲了自己为什么偏好那个方向，结果发现两个人比其他人都在意得多。于是我们就用了他们的想法，其余人在细节上让步。先听对方说，通常比争半天管用。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":4,"en":"To some extent, yes. Your twenties are when you pick a career, a partner, maybe a city to settle in, and those set the direction of your whole life. But I wouldn't say it's all over after that. My uncle changed careers entirely at forty and was much happier. So the big decisions cluster in your twenties, but you can still redirect things later if you're brave enough.","cn":"某种程度上是的。二十多岁的时候你要选职业、选伴侣、也许还选定居的城市，这些基本定了你一生的方向。但我不觉得过了这阶段就定型了。我叔叔四十岁时彻底转了行，反而开心多了。所以重大决定确实集中在二十多岁，但只要你够有勇气，之后照样能重新调整方向。"},{"b":"02","c":22,"i":5,"en":"I'd say it depends on the stakes. For something small and reversible, quick is better — you don't want to waste a week choosing a phone case. But for big, life-changing calls, going slowly and gathering information usually pays off. Rushing into a house purchase or a job offer can cost you dearly. So my rule is: fast when it's cheap to fix, slow when it isn't.","cn":"我觉得得看这事有多重。小事、能反悔的，快一点更好——没必要为一款手机壳纠结一星期。但大事、会改变人生的，慢慢来、多收集信息通常更划算。买房或者接offer这种，冲动决定代价会很大。所以我的原则是：好改的事就快，不好改的就慢。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":0,"en":"Mainly people in the public eye, I'd say. Celebrities, politicians, athletes and business leaders get interviewed all the time, especially when they've got a new film or product to promote. Ordinary people get interviewed too in a way — think of job candidates, or witnesses on the news. So really, anyone whose story or opinion might interest an audience.","cn":"主要是公众人物吧。明星、政客、运动员、商界领袖经常被采访，尤其是新片或者新产品要宣传的时候。其实普通人某种程度也会被采访——比如求职者，或者新闻里的目击者。所以说到底，只要一个人的故事或观点可能吸引观众，就有人去访。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":1,"en":"I think it's often about control and nerves. Some people worry their words will be twisted or taken out of context, so they'd rather stay quiet. Others just hate being in the spotlight — my friend froze completely at a company interview and has avoided them since. And a few have something to hide, so they dodge questions on purpose. So the reasons vary a lot.","cn":"我觉得常常是出于控制感和紧张。有些人怕自己的话被曲解、被断章取义，所以宁愿不说。也有人就是不喜欢被聚焦——我朋友在公司面试时彻底卡壳，之后就躲着面试。还有少数人是心里有鬼，故意回避问题。所以原因挺多的。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":2,"en":"I'd say it's usually because they've had bad experiences. If an interview drags on with pointless questions, or the interviewer clearly hasn't done any homework, it feels like a total waste. My cousin went to one where the manager just read generic questions off a sheet, and she came out annoyed. Done well, though, an interview saves everyone time by checking if it's a good fit.","cn":"我觉得通常是因为他们有过糟糕的经历。如果面试拖拖拉拉、净问些没用的问题，或者面试官明显没做任何功课，就会让人觉得很浪费时间。我表姐遇到过一次，经理就照着纸念些套话，她出来一肚子气。但面试做得好，其实能通过确认合不合适来帮大家省时间。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":3,"en":"First, research the company properly — its products, values and recent news — so you can ask smart questions. Then prepare a few stories that show your skills, using real examples rather than vague claims. My sister practises out loud in front of a mirror, which sounds silly but really helps. Also, think about your weaknesses in advance, and always prepare a couple of questions to ask them back.","cn":"首先，把公司好好研究一遍——产品、价值观、最近的新闻——这样你才能问出有水平的问题。然后准备几个能体现你能力的故事，多用真实例子，别空泛地吹。我姐姐会对着镜子大声练，听着傻，但真管用。还有，提前想想自己的短板，另外一定准备两三个反向提问的问题。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":4,"en":"Mostly safe, polished things, I'd say. They tend to thank everyone, talk about hard work and passion, and mention how grateful they are for their team. It's a bit of a script, honestly, because saying anything controversial can blow up online. Occasionally one will drop a genuine, personal story that feels real, and those are the interviews people actually remember.","cn":"多半是说些稳妥、滴水不漏的话。他们一般会感谢大家，聊聊努力和热爱，再表达对团队的感激。说实话，这有点像念稿子，因为说点有争议的随时会在网上翻车。偶尔有人会讲一段真实、私人的故事，那种才让人觉得真诚，也是大家真正记得住的采访。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":5,"en":"First, tailor your CV to each job instead of sending the same one everywhere. Then apply through the right channels — company websites, referrals, even LinkedIn. Networking helps a lot; my brother got his interview through a former classmate who put in a good word. Finally, a short, friendly cover note that shows you've actually read the job ad makes a real difference.","cn":"首先，针对每个岗位改简历，别一份简历到处投。然后走对的渠道去投——公司官网、内推，甚至领英。人脉也很关键；我哥的面试机会就是老同学帮忙说了句好话得来的。最后，写一封简短、友好的求职信，让人看出你确实读过招聘要求，这真的会加分很多。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":6,"en":"Because a CV only shows facts on paper, while an interview reveals how someone actually thinks and communicates. You can't tell from a résumé whether a person stays calm under pressure or gets along with a team — those things show up in conversation. It also lets both sides check the fit. So interviews are really about seeing the person behind the qualifications, not just the qualifications.","cn":"因为简历只展示纸面上的事实，而面试能看出一个人到底怎么思考、怎么沟通。你从简历上看不出一个人抗压能力如何、和团队合不合得来——这些都要在对话里才体现出来。面试也让双方互相确认合不合适。所以说白了，面试看的是资历背后那个真实的人，而不只是资历本身。"},{"b":"02","c":23,"i":7,"en":"I'd say smart but a bit conservative. Something like a plain shirt with dark trousers or a modest dress works for almost any office job, and it signals respect without showing off. My cousin once turned up in trainers and a hoodie to a bank interview and instantly felt out of place. Still, for creative or tech roles, you can dress more casually — just check the company's culture first.","cn":"我会说穿得得体但稍微保守一点。像素色衬衫配深色长裤，或者一条端庄的连衣裙，几乎适合任何办公室岗位，既表示尊重又不张扬。我表哥有次穿运动鞋加卫衣去面试银行，一进去就觉得自己格格不入。不过创意或科技类的岗位可以随意些——先了解下公司文化就行。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":0,"en":"These days it's mostly entertainment and everyday stuff. My younger cousins talk nonstop about games, music, short videos and which cafe to visit next. A lot of conversation happens online too — memes, clips, gossip about celebrities. Careers and studying do come up, but usually only when exams are close. So I'd say fun topics dominate, and serious chats are more occasional.","cn":"现在主要是娱乐和日常的事。我那些小辈的亲戚张口闭口就是游戏、音乐、短视频，还有下次去哪家咖啡店。很多聊天也发生在网上——表情包、视频片段、明星八卦。事业和学习也会聊，但一般只在考试临近时。所以我觉得娱乐话题占大头，正经的聊天反而少一些。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":1,"en":"Well, it usually causes problems. If one person keeps talking and the other is zoning out, the speaker eventually notices and feels hurt or ignored, which can damage the relationship. Worse, you might miss something important — like instructions at work. My colleague once half-listened during a handover and ended up making a silly mistake. So listening properly actually protects both the talk and the bond.","cn":"呃，通常会引起问题。如果一个人一直在说，另一个人却在走神，说话的人最后会察觉，觉得受伤或者被忽视，关系就会受影响。更糟的是，你可能漏掉重要信息——比如工作上的交接。我同事有一次交接时没认真听，结果犯了个低级错误。所以好好听，其实既保护了这次交流，也保护了感情。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":2,"en":"There are a few signs. They keep eye contact, nod at the right moments, and ask follow-up questions instead of just saying 'yeah'. Also, they usually remember what you said later. My best chats are with people who circle back — 'you mentioned your exam, how did it go?'. If someone keeps checking their phone or glancing away, that's a pretty clear signal they've checked out.","cn":"有几个迹象。他们会保持眼神接触，在该点头的时候点头，还会追问细节，而不只是应一句'嗯'。另外，他们通常之后还能记得你说过的话。我聊得最舒服的对象，是那种会回头来问的人——'你上次提过考试，考得怎么样？'。要是有人一直看手机或者眼神飘走，那就是很明显的信号：人已经走神了。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":3,"en":"A teacher can read a lot from body language — who's leaning forward, who's doodling, whose eyes have gone glassy. Asking questions is the real test, especially open ones instead of yes-or-no. My favourite teacher used to randomly call on people, which kept everyone alert. She'd also pause and let the silence work, because the ones who weren't following would suddenly look up.","cn":"老师能从肢体语言看出很多东西——谁往前倾，谁在涂鸦，谁的眼神发直。提问才是真正的检验，尤其要用开放性问题，而不是是非题。我最喜欢的老师会随机点名，让所有人都不敢松懈。她还会故意停顿、让沉默发挥作用，因为没跟上的人会突然抬头。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":4,"en":"Honestly, I think it's valuable but tricky. Talking with people who see things differently forces you to defend your views, so you either strengthen your argument or change your mind — both are good. But it can also turn into a fight if neither side is willing to listen. My rule is to stay curious and ask why, rather than trying to win the argument. That usually keeps things friendly.","cn":"说实话，我觉得这很有价值，但也不好把握。和看法不同的人聊天，会逼着你为自己的观点辩护，于是你要么把论点磨得更扎实，要么改变想法——两种都是收获。但如果双方都不肯听，就容易变成吵架。我的原则是保持好奇、多问为什么，而不是非争个输赢。这样一般就能聊得还算融洽。"},{"b":"02","c":24,"i":5,"en":"I'd say it varies a lot. Some young people are really close to their grandparents and chat with them for hours, especially in smaller towns where families live nearby. But many others find the topics too different — the elderly talk about health and the past, while the young care about trends and tech. So it happens, but it takes patience from both sides to keep the conversation going.","cn":"我觉得差别挺大的。有些年轻人跟祖辈很亲，能聊好几个小时，尤其是在家人住得近的小城镇。但很多人觉得话题差太远——老人聊健康和过去，年轻人关心潮流和科技。所以这种聊天是有的，但需要双方都有耐心才能聊得下去。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":0,"en":"Before school, I'd say the basic ones: speaking clearly, following simple instructions, and looking after themselves a bit — like dressing and washing hands. Sharing and taking turns matter too, because they make school life easier. My nephew learned to say 'please' and 'sorry' before he could read, and it changed how other kids treated him. So social skills are as important as academic ones.","cn":"上学前，我会说是那些基础技能：把话说清楚、听懂简单的指令，还有稍微能照顾自己——比如穿衣、洗手。懂得分享和轮流也很重要，因为能让学校生活顺利些。我侄子还没认字就学会了说'请'和'对不起'，这直接改变了其他小朋友对他的态度。所以社交技能和学业技能一样重要。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":1,"en":"They're quite different. In a group, you can learn from others, ask questions on the spot, and get pushed along by the pace, which is motivating. But you can also fall behind or hide in the crowd. Learning alone suits people who like going at their own speed and focusing deeply. I learned to swim in a class but taught myself guitar, and honestly each worked for different reasons.","cn":"差别挺大的。在小组里，你能向别人学、当场提问，还被集体的节奏推着往前走，很有动力。但你也可能被落下，或者躲在人群里不出力。而自学适合那种喜欢按自己节奏走、想深入专注的人。我游泳是跟班学的，吉他却是自学的，说实话各有各的用处。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":2,"en":"The biggest difference is probably pace and patience. Kids absorb things quickly and aren't afraid of sounding silly, so they just copy and try again until they get it. Adults think more about rules and mistakes, which slows them down and makes them self-conscious. But adults compensate with focus and self-discipline. So children may learn faster, while adults often learn more carefully.","cn":"最大的区别大概是速度和心态。小孩子吸收得快，也不怕说错丢人，所以就是模仿、再来一次，直到学会。成年人会更多地想规矩、在意犯错，于是学得慢，还容易不好意思。但成年人靠专注和自律来弥补。所以孩子可能学得快，而成年人往往学得更细。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":3,"en":"In many ways, yes. Children have more free time, less fear of failure, and brains that are still soaking up patterns, so they pick up things like languages almost effortlessly. My niece learned to ride a bike in an afternoon. But it's not a hard rule — adults have better focus and can study more systematically. So it's easier for kids to start, but harder for them to persist without support.","cn":"很多方面是的。孩子空闲时间更多，没那么怕失败，大脑也还在大量吸收规律，所以像学语言这种事几乎是毫不费力。我侄女一个下午就学会了骑自行车。但这不是铁律——成年人专注力更好，能更系统地学习。所以孩子起步更容易，但没有支持的话，坚持下来反而更难。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":4,"en":"Definitely, and often more effectively than from an adult. Kids explain things in their own simple language and demonstrate rather than lecture, so it feels less intimidating. My cousin learned to skateboard by copying the older boys in the neighbourhood — no lesson, just watching and copying. Peers also create healthy competition, which keeps them practising. So children can be great teachers to each other.","cn":"绝对可以，而且往往比大人教得还有效。孩子会用自己简单的语言来讲解，示范而不是说教，所以没那么吓人。我表弟就是照着小区里大点的男孩学会滑板的——没人正式教，就是看和模仿。同伴之间还会形成良性竞争，让大家一直练下去。所以孩子之间可以互相教得很好。"},{"b":"02","c":25,"i":5,"en":"Yes, I'd say so, though maybe not the 'specialised' part. Everyone benefits from being genuinely good at one or two things, because that gives you confidence and a way to stand out — whether it's coding, cooking or fixing cars. My uncle is the go-to person for plumbing in our whole street. But I don't think you need to master just one thing; having a few solid skills is often more useful.","cn":"是的，我觉得是，不过可能不在于'专'这个字。每个人都从真正擅长一两件事里受益，因为这能给你自信，也让你有立足之地——不管是编程、做饭还是修车。我叔叔就是整条街修水管都找他。但我不觉得非得只精通一件事；有几项扎实的技能往往更实用。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":0,"en":"I'd say quite a lot of people can, at least the younger generation. Most students here learn English from primary school to university, and many pick up a second one like Japanese or Korean because they're crazy about the pop culture. My cousin studied Japanese for three years just to watch anime without subtitles. Still, many of us can read but freeze up when we have to speak.","cn":"我觉得挺多的，至少年轻一代是这样。这边大部分学生从小学到大学一直学英语，还有很多人会再学一门比如日语或韩语，因为特别迷那边的流行文化。我表妹学了三年日语，就为了看动漫不用字幕。不过我们很多人能读，但真要开口说的时候就卡壳了。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":1,"en":"Yes, definitely, especially in big cities. If you work in trade or tourism, speaking another language is almost a must -- my aunt works at a hotel and gets paid extra just because she speaks English well. It opens doors, I mean you can deal with foreign clients directly instead of relying on translators. That said, for a lot of local jobs like teaching or cooking, it honestly doesn't make much difference.","cn":"是的，肯定有帮助，尤其是在大城市。如果你做外贸或旅游，会说另一门语言几乎是必须的——我姑姑在酒店工作，就因为英语好，工资能拿得更高。它会打开机会，我的意思是你能直接对接外国客户，不用靠翻译。不过话说回来，像教书、做菜这种本地工作，说实话区别也不大。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":2,"en":"Yeah, more and more people are branching out beyond English. Japanese and Korean are really popular with students here because of music and dramas, and a fair number of people study French or German for studying or working abroad. My neighbour actually picked up Spanish on her own through an app, just for fun. So I'd say English is still the default, but it's definitely not the only one people bother with anymore.","cn":"是的，越来越多人不再只学英语了。因为音乐和电视剧，日语和韩语在学生里特别火，还有不少人为了出国留学或工作去学法语、德语。我邻居其实就是自己用App自学了西班牙语，纯粹图个乐。所以我觉得英语还是默认选择，但肯定不再是大家唯一愿意花心思学的语言了。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":3,"en":"I think it's mostly about how their brains work. Kids just soak things up like a sponge, and they're not afraid of making mistakes -- my little nephew will happily repeat a silly word twenty times without feeling embarrassed. Adults, on the other hand, overthink everything and worry about looking stupid. Plus children have way more free time, so they can practise all day, while we're stuck at work.","cn":"我觉得主要是因为大脑的工作方式不同。小孩就像海绵一样吸收东西，而且不怕犯错——我小侄子会开开心心地把一个傻乎乎的词重复二十遍也不觉得尴尬。而大人呢，什么都想太多，还担心出丑。再加上小孩空闲时间多得多，可以整天练，而我们得上班。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":4,"en":"I'd say there are two main ways. One is by copying others -- kids learn to talk just by listening to their parents, and honestly that's how I picked up cooking, just watching my mum. The other is by trial and error, actually trying something, messing up, and adjusting. When I learned guitar I failed for weeks before it clicked. I think the hands-on way sticks better, but a bit of both works best.","cn":"我觉得主要有两种方式。一种是通过模仿别人——小孩就是听父母说话学会说话的，说实话我学做饭也是这么来的，就看着我妈妈做。另一种是试错，真的去试，搞砸了，再调整。我学吉他的时候失败了好几个星期才突然开窍。我觉得亲手实践的方式记得更牢，但两者结合效果最好。"},{"b":"04","c":0,"i":5,"en":"To be honest, I think consistency beats talent. You can have the best teacher and the fanciest app, but if you only study once a week it won't stick. My friend learned fluent Spanish just by chatting with people for ten minutes every single day. And of course you have to actually use it, not just memorise grammar rules -- speaking badly and being corrected is how you really improve.","cn":"说实话，我觉得坚持比天赋更重要。你可能有最好的老师和最花哨的App，但如果一周只学一次，还是记不住。我朋友就靠每天跟人聊十分钟，把西班牙语说得很流利。当然你还得真去用它，不能光背语法规则——说得烂然后被人纠正，才是你真正进步的方式。"},{"b":"04","c":1,"i":0,"en":"For a few, yes, but honestly not with all of them. I still see my best friend from primary school every couple of months, and when we meet it feels like no time has passed. But with most of the others we just follow each other online and swap the odd message. I think you keep the ones who knew you before you became 'proper', if that makes sense -- the rest just drift.","cn":"有几个人还联系，但说实话不是所有都联系。我每隔几个月还会跟小学时最好的朋友见一面，见面的时候感觉时间好像没走过一样。但其他大多数人，我们就只是在网上互相关注、偶尔发条消息。我觉得你会留下那些在你'还没长成样子'之前就认识你的人，这么说能理解吧——剩下的就慢慢淡了。"},{"b":"04","c":1,"i":1,"en":"I'd say it's really important, maybe more than people admit. As a kid you're still figuring out who you are, and your friends are the ones who make you feel normal when everything at home feels confusing. I remember being terrified on my first day at a new school, and one girl just sat with me at lunch -- that tiny thing made the whole year bearable. It teaches you how to trust and share.","cn":"我觉得这真的很重要，可能比大家承认的还要重要。小时候你还在弄明白自己是谁，而当你觉得家里一切都让你困惑的时候，是朋友让你觉得自己是正常的。我记得转到新学校第一天特别害怕，有个女生中午就坐到我旁边——就这么件小事，让那整一年都变得能熬下去了。它教会你怎么去信任和分享。"},{"b":"04","c":1,"i":2,"en":"It's a double-edged sword, to be honest. On the plus side, it's brilliant for keeping in touch -- my group chat with old classmates is still active after ten years, and we share photos and jokes all the time. But I do think it can make friendship a bit shallow, because people just 'like' your post instead of actually calling you when you're down. So it's convenient, but it can't replace a real chat.","cn":"说实话，这是把双刃剑。好的一面是，它特别适合保持联系——我跟老同学的群聊十年了还活跃着，我们总在里面发照片、开玩笑。但我确实觉得它会让友谊变得有点浮于表面，因为你难过的时候，人家只是给你点个赞，而不是真的打电话给你。所以它很方便，但替代不了一次真正的聊天。"},{"b":"04","c":1,"i":3,"en":"They give you different things, I think. Older friends are great when you need advice -- my friend who's ten years older has talked me out of plenty of bad decisions, and she's just calmer about life. Younger friends keep you fun and up to date, they drag you to try new things. But the age gap can be awkward, like you don't get each other's music. Honestly it's nice to have both.","cn":"我觉得他们给你的是不同的东西。年长的朋友在你需要建议的时候特别有用——我那个比我大十岁的朋友劝住过我好多次糟糕的决定，她对生活就是更淡定。年轻的朋友则让你保持有趣、跟得上潮流，会拉着你去尝试新东西。但年龄差有时候也尴尬，比如你们get不到彼此的音乐。说实话，两种都有是最好的。"},{"b":"04","c":1,"i":4,"en":"Definitely, and not always for the better. On one hand it's never been easier to stay close -- I video-call my friend abroad every week, which would've been impossible for my parents' generation. On the other hand, I've noticed people hanging out together but all staring at their phones, so the friendship feels half-there. I mean, we text constantly but sometimes forget how to just sit and have a proper conversation.","cn":"肯定变了，而且不总是往好的方向。一方面，保持亲密从来没这么容易过——我每周都跟国外的朋友视频通话，这在我父母那代根本不可能。另一方面，我注意到大家明明一起出去玩，却都在盯着手机，友谊就感觉只到一半。我的意思是，我们天天发消息，但有时候忘了该怎么坐下来好好聊一次天。"},{"b":"04","c":2,"i":0,"en":"I'd say there are quite a few. The obvious one is that you know exactly what you're eating -- no chemicals, no mystery. My mum grows tomatoes on our balcony and honestly they taste a million times better than the ones from the supermarket. It also saves money and it's surprisingly relaxing, just watering them after work. The downside is it takes time and patience, and you can't grow much in a small flat.","cn":"我觉得好处不少。最明显的是你知道自己吃的到底是什么——没有化学东西，没有不明成分。我妈妈在阳台上种番茄，说实话比超市买的好吃太多了。它还能省钱，而且意外地很解压，下班回来浇浇水就行。缺点是费时费力，而且在很小的公寓里也种不了多少。"},{"b":"04","c":2,"i":1,"en":"More than before, I'd say, but it's still a minority. Older people in the countryside definitely do it, and lots of families with a little garden grow vegetables or flowers. But in big cities, most people live in flats and just don't have the space. My grandma grows all her own greens, while my city colleagues think I'm odd for having herbs on the windowsill. So it's growing, but not the norm yet.","cn":"我觉得比以前多了，但还是少数。乡下的老人肯定种，很多有个小院子的家庭也会种菜种花。但在大城市，大多数人住公寓，就是没空间。我奶奶自己种所有的青菜，而我在城里的同事觉得我在窗台上养几盆香草很奇怪。所以是在变多，但还不是主流。"},{"b":"04","c":2,"i":2,"en":"Depends what you mean by easy. The low-maintenance stuff, like herbs or a cactus, is almost impossible to kill -- my mint survived me forgetting it for two weeks. But anything serious, like tomatoes or roses, needs real care: the right soil, water, sunlight. I tried to grow strawberries once and they died within days. So I'd say it's easy to start, but hard to do well.","cn":"要看你怎么定义容易。像香草或者仙人掌这种好养的，几乎想养死都难——我那片薄荷我忘了它两个星期都还活着。但像番茄、玫瑰这种正经的，就需要精心照顾了：合适的土、水、阳光。我有次试着种草莓，没几天就死了。所以我觉得入门容易，但要种好很难。"},{"b":"04","c":2,"i":3,"en":"I think it's partly about slowing down. In a busy city life everything's fast and digital, so watching something grow gives you a bit of calm and control. My uncle says gardening is his therapy after work. And there's real pride in eating something you grew yourself -- even one little pepper feels like a win. For older people, I think it's also a habit and a link to how they grew up.","cn":"我觉得一部分是为了慢下来。在城市里忙忙碌碌，一切都很快、很数字化，所以看着什么东西生长会给你一点平静和掌控感。我叔叔说种花草是他下班后的疗愈。而且吃到自己种的东西会有真正的成就感——哪怕就一个小辣椒也感觉很值。对老人来说，我觉得这也是一种习惯，一种跟他们成长方式之间的联系。"},{"b":"04","c":2,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I actually think it should be part of the curriculum. It teaches kids where food really comes from -- a lot of them think vegetables just appear in the supermarket. My school had a tiny plot and we grew sunflowers, and I learned more about patience and responsibility from that than from any lecture. It's also hands-on, so even restless kids get into it. Just make it fun, not a chore.","cn":"是的，我其实觉得它应该成为课程的一部分。它能教孩子食物到底从哪来——很多孩子以为蔬菜就是从超市里冒出来的。我学校有一小块地，我们种向日葵，我从那儿学到的关于耐心和责任的东西比听任何讲座都多。而且它是动手的，所以哪怕坐不住的孩子也会喜欢。只要别弄成负担，让它好玩就行。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":0,"en":"I think it's usually a mix of freedom and frustration. A lot of people are just tired of answering to a boss, or they have an idea they really believe in and want to run it their own way. My cousin quit his office job to open a coffee shop because he hated being told what to do. Of course money matters too. So it's often independence first, profit second.","cn":"我觉得通常是自由和不满的混合。很多人就是受够了听老板的，或者他们有个自己特别相信的想法，想按自己的方式去做。我表哥辞掉办公室工作去开咖啡店，就是因为讨厌被人指来指去。当然钱也重要。所以往往是自由第一，赚钱第二。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":1,"en":"It depends on the start-up, honestly. For small local businesses, a bit of help could be great -- maybe cheap loans or lower taxes for the first year, so they survive that tough beginning. But I'm wary of just handing out cash, because plenty of people would waste it. I've seen a friend's bakery fail even with a grant. So targeted support like training or tax breaks works better than plain money.","cn":"说实话，要看是什么创业公司。对小型的本地生意，给一点帮助会很好——比如低息贷款，或者第一年减税，让它们熬过艰难的开头。但我不太赞成直接发钱，因为很多人会浪费掉。我见过一个朋友的面包店即使拿了补助还是倒闭了。所以像培训、减税这种有针对性的支持，比直接给钱更有效。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":2,"en":"I'd say it really divides people. Big stores win on choice and price -- you can get everything in one trip and things are often cheaper, which is why my parents do their weekly shop at a huge supermarket. But plenty of people, especially older ones, prefer the small corner shop because it's closer and the owner knows them. Honestly, though, most young people just shop online now, so both kinds are struggling.","cn":"我觉得这很因人而异。大店胜在种类和价格——一趟就能买齐所有东西，而且通常更便宜，所以我爸妈每周都去大超市采购。但很多人，尤其是老人，更喜欢街角小店，因为近，而且店主认识他们。不过说实话，现在大多数年轻人都在网上买东西，所以两种店都挺难的。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":3,"en":"I'd boil it down to a few things. First, you have to actually solve a problem people have, not just have a nice idea. Second, timing and location matter a lot -- my neighbour opened a bubble tea shop right next to a school, and it was packed from day one. And third, I think good service is underrated; people go back where staff treat them well. Luck plays a part, but those basics are key.","cn":"我会归结为几点。首先，你得真的解决人们存在的某个问题，而不只是有个好想法。其次，时机和地点很重要——我邻居把奶茶店开在了一所学校旁边，从第一天起就爆满。第三，我觉得好的服务被低估了；人们会回到那些员工对他们好的地方。运气也有一定作用，但这些基本功是关键。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":4,"en":"Usually it's not one big thing but a slow slide. Cash flow is the classic killer -- a business can look healthy and still die because customers pay late or a quiet season hits. My uncle's restaurant closed because the rent kept rising and he just couldn't keep up. Then there's ignoring what customers want; if you refuse to change, people go elsewhere. And bad timing, like opening right before a recession, can finish you off.","cn":"通常不是某一件大事，而是一步步慢慢垮掉。现金流是经典的杀手——一个生意看起来挺健康，但还是会死，因为客户付款晚，或者淡季来了。我叔叔的餐厅关了，就是因为房租一直涨，他实在跟不上了。然后是无视客户的需求；如果你拒绝改变，人家就去别家了。还有就是时机不好，比如正好在经济衰退前开业，能直接把你干倒。"},{"b":"04","c":3,"i":5,"en":"Honestly, it's gotten easier in some ways and harder in others. Easier because registering a company online now takes a day or two, and you can sell nationwide through e-commerce -- my friend runs a shop from her bedroom. But harder because the market is so crowded and competition is brutal. Rent in cities is sky-high too. So the paperwork is easy; surviving the first two years is the hard part.","cn":"说实话，有些方面变容易了，有些方面更难了。说容易，是因为现在网上注册公司一两天就搞定，你还能通过电商卖到全国——我朋友就在自己卧室里经营一家店。但更难是因为市场太挤，竞争太残酷了。城里的房租也贵得离谱。所以手续是简单了；难的是熬过头两年。"},{"b":"04","c":4,"i":0,"en":"I'd say it depends on the company and the industry. For strategic ones, like tech or clean energy, some support makes sense because they create jobs and push the country forward. My dad works for a solar firm that got tax breaks and it genuinely helped. But for big profitable companies that are already doing fine, I don't think they need a handout. So targeted help, yes; blank cheques, no.","cn":"我觉得要看公司和行业。对战略性行业，比如科技或清洁能源，给一些支持是合理的，因为它们创造就业、推动国家往前走。我爸在一家太阳能公司，拿到了税收减免，确实帮了大忙。但对已经运转良好、很赚钱的大公司，我觉得它们不需要被救济。所以有针对性的帮助，可以；空白支票，不行。"},{"b":"04","c":4,"i":1,"en":"Yes, I think they should, but as a responsibility, not a favour. Big companies make money from the community -- from local workers and customers -- so giving some back is only fair. Many here do sponsor schools or disaster relief. That said, I don't like it when it's just a PR stunt with a huge logo on it. So yes to donating, if it's genuine and not filmed for show.","cn":"是的，我觉得应该，但这是责任，不是施舍。大公司从社区赚钱——靠当地的员工和顾客——所以回馈一些才公平。这边很多公司会赞助学校或者救灾。话虽如此，我不喜欢那种只是为了公关、上面印着巨大logo的作秀。所以捐赠是好事，前提是真心，而不是拍给别人看。"},{"b":"04","c":4,"i":2,"en":"Absolutely, I'd say it's the whole point. A company can have the best product, but if customers aren't happy they'll switch in a second, and word spreads fast online now. I once had terrible service at a phone shop and I told everyone I knew. On the flip side, my favourite noodle place remembers my order, and I go back constantly even though it's not cheap. So keeping customers happy basically keeps the business alive.","cn":"绝对重要，我觉得这就是根本。一家公司可以有最好的产品，但如果顾客不满意，他们马上就会换，而且现在网上的口碑传得飞快。我有次在手机店遇到特别差的服务，我告诉了所有认识的人。反过来，我最爱的那家面馆记得我点什么，我就老去，哪怕它不便宜。所以让顾客满意基本上就是让生意活下去。"},{"b":"04","c":4,"i":3,"en":"On the whole, yes, I think so. Medicine saves lives and protects everyone during a crisis, so money spent on it is money well spent. But I would not put it all into one area — training enough doctors and nurses matters as much as buying machines. If a country spends billons on new hospitals but has no staff to run them, the money is wasted.","cn":"总体上是应该的。医疗能救命，而且遇到危机时保护所有人，所以花在医疗上的钱花得值。但我不会把钱全砸在一个地方——培养足够的医生护士，和买设备一样重要。如果一个国家花几十亿建新医院，却没有人员去运转，那钱就浪费了。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":0,"en":"I'd say it's genuinely hard, and people underestimate it. It's not just long hours -- though my cousin, who's a junior doctor, often works night shifts and barely sleeps. It's also the emotional weight. You're dealing with scared patients and sometimes life-or-death calls, and one mistake can follow you forever. Studying alone takes years. So yes, it's rewarding, but calling it 'easy' would be a joke.","cn":"我觉得是真的难，而且大家往往低估了。不只是工作时间长——我表姐是个住院医生，经常上夜班，几乎睡不了觉。还有情感上的压力。你要面对害怕的病人，有时还要做生死攸关的决定，而一个错误可能会跟你一辈子。光是读书就要好多年。所以是的，它很有意义，但说它'容易'就纯属开玩笑了。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":1,"en":"I think it can be, if it's taught in the right way. Kids are naturally fascinated by living things and gross stuff -- my little brother was obsessed with how a heart pumps blood, he watched videos for hours. The problem is when it turns into pure memorisation of Latin names and diagrams. Tied to real life, like why we get sick or how vaccines work, biology is one of the most interesting subjects there is.","cn":"我觉得如果教得好，是很有意思的。孩子天生就对活着的东西和有点恶心的话题着迷——我弟弟特别迷心脏怎么泵血，能看视频看几个小时。问题在于它变成了纯粹背拉丁学名和图解。要是跟现实联系起来，比如我们为什么会生病、疫苗怎么起作用，生物就是最有趣的科目之一了。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":2,"en":"I think there are a few reasons. Some have a personal story -- a grandparent got sick and the doctor saved them, so they want to do the same. Others are drawn to the respect and stability, because doctors here are well thought of and it's a secure career. And honestly, some parents push them into it, since medicine looks prestigious. My friend wanted to be a doctor until she did the unpaid training.","cn":"我觉得有几个原因。有些人是有一段个人经历——爷爷奶奶生病了，医生救了他们，所以想去做同样的事。另一些人被尊重和稳定吸引，因为在这边医生很受尊敬，也是份有保障的职业。说实话，还有些是父母推着他们去的，因为医学显得有面子。我朋友本来想当医生，直到体会到那种无薪的培训阶段。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":3,"en":"Yes, I strongly think so, and I'd argue it pays off. Medical research is expensive and slow, but it saves far more than it costs -- think of how vaccines wiped out diseases that used to kill millions. My grandma's cancer treatment only exists because of decades of funded research. Government money also matters because private companies chase profit and ignore rare diseases. So yes, it's money well spent, even if results take years.","cn":"是的，我强烈觉得应该，而且我认为这很值。医学研究又贵又慢，但它省下的远远超过花掉的——想想疫苗消灭了多少过去能杀死数百万人的疾病。我奶奶的癌症治疗之所以存在，就是因为几十年的政府资助研究。政府出钱也很重要，因为私营公司追逐利润，会忽略罕见病。所以是的，这钱花得值，哪怕出成果要等好多年。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":4,"en":"I would say yes, though not every minute of it. Planning ahead saves money and stress, and it is how you avoid turning up to find everything booked. That said, leaving a little space free is wise, because the best moments on a trip are often unplanned.","cn":"我觉得有好处，但不必每分钟都规划好。提前规划能省钱、省心，也能避免到了才发现全订满了。不过留一点空档是明智的，因为旅行中最美好的时刻往往都是计划之外的。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":5,"en":"Mainly because tourism brings in money. Visitors spend on hotels, food and transport, which creates jobs and supports small businesses. For some places, a single busy season can carry the whole local economy for a year, so attracting tourists is really about protecting livelihoods.","cn":"主要是因为旅游能带来收入。游客在住宿、餐饮和交通上花钱，这会创造工作、养活小生意。对有些地方来说，一个旺季就能撑起当地一整年的经济，所以吸引游客其实是在保住大家的生计。"},{"b":"04","c":5,"i":6,"en":"Most people are led by two things: money and time. School holidays and public holidays decide when they can go, while prices decide where — if flights are cheap in a certain month, that is when they book. Others go by weather, or by when their friends are free.","cn":"大多数人受两件事支配：钱和时间。学校的假期和公共假日决定他们什么时候能去，而价格决定去哪——如果某个月机票便宜，他们就会那时候订。也有人看天气，或者看朋友什么时候有空。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":0,"en":"I'd say people here mostly go to two kinds of places: famous scenic spots or somewhere by the sea. During the big holidays like Spring Festival, half the country seems to head to the beaches in the south or the ancient towns in the mountains. Others just go back to their hometown to see family, which counts as a holiday too. It's often crowded, but people don't seem to mind.","cn":"我觉得这边的人主要去两种地方：著名景点或者海边。像春节这样的大假期，感觉半个国家都涌向南方海滩或者山里的古镇。也有不少人干脆回老家看亲戚，那也算一种度假。常常很挤，但大家好像也不太在意。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":1,"en":"Young people are a bit different, I think. They love trendy spots -- the 'internet-famous' cafes, the photo spots you see on social media, music festivals, that kind of thing. My younger cousin plans whole trips around where she can get good photos. A lot also go abroad if they can afford it, like to Thailand or Japan, since it's exciting and not too far. So it's less about relaxing and more about the experience.","cn":"我觉得年轻人有点不一样。他们喜欢潮的地方——那种'网红'咖啡馆、社交媒体上能拍照的地点、音乐节之类的。我表妹会围绕哪里能拍出好照片来规划整趟旅行。很多人在负担得起的时候还会出国，比如去泰国或日本，因为又刺激又不太远。所以对他们来说，重点不是放松，而是体验。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":2,"en":"For me, about a week to ten days is ideal. Short enough that you don't get bored or fall behind at work, long enough to actually switch off and settle into a place. A two-day trip always feels rushed, you spend more time travelling than enjoying. But honestly, more than two weeks and I start missing home. So a week, maybe with a couple of days buffer, is my sweet spot.","cn":"对我来说，大概一周到十天最理想。短到不会觉得无聊或者落下工作，长到能真正放松、慢下来待在一个地方。两天的行程总感觉太赶，路上花的时间比玩的还多。但说实话，超过两周我就开始想家了。所以一周，也许再留个两三天缓冲，就是我的最佳时长。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":3,"en":"People do it pretty differently, I've noticed. Some are super organised -- they book flights and hotels months ahead and make a plan for each day, that's my mum. Others, like my friend, just pick a city, fly there, and figure it out on the spot. Most people are in between: book the big stuff early, then leave the days free. Travel apps have made the research part way easier.","cn":"我注意到大家规划方式差别挺大。有些人特别有条理——提前几个月订机票酒店，还安排好每天干什么，我妈就是这样。另一些人，像我朋友，就选个城市，飞过去，到了再临时决定。大多数人介于两者之间：大件提前订，然后白天留空。旅行App让做攻略这部分容易多了。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":4,"en":"Yes, definitely, especially for anything big or far away. If you don't book early you'll pay double or end up with nowhere to stay, and nobody wants that. Planning ahead also saves stress -- my parents once turned up in a city during a festival with no hotel booked, and it was a nightmare. That said, don't over-plan, or you just tick off a list and miss the fun of wandering.","cn":"是的，绝对重要，尤其是大行程或者远地方。不早点订，要么花双倍的钱，要么根本没地方住，没人想那样。提前规划还能避免压力——我爸妈有次在一个节庆期间到了某个城市却没订酒店，那真是场灾难。话虽如此，别规划过头，否则你只是在打勾清单，错过了闲逛的乐趣。"},{"b":"04","c":6,"i":5,"en":"It's mostly about money, I'd say. Tourism brings in cash, creates jobs in hotels, restaurants and transport, and it's a kind of export you don't have to ship anywhere. For a small town with few industries, a few beautiful sights can be the whole economy -- I saw that in an old village that survives on visitors. There's also soft power: when tourists come and go home with good memories, it improves the country's image.","cn":"我觉得主要还是为了钱。旅游带来收入，在酒店、餐饮、交通上创造就业，而且它是一种不用运出去的出口。对一个工业很少的小镇来说，几个漂亮的景点就能撑起整个经济——我在一个靠游客过活的古村就看到了这一点。还有软实力：游客来了，带着好印象回去，就提升了一个国家的形象。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I'd say visiting each other's homes is still a big deal here, though maybe a bit less than before. For close friends and relatives it's really normal -- you just drop by, have tea, stay for dinner. My mum's friends come over almost every weekend. But for casual friends or colleagues, people increasingly meet at a restaurant or cafe instead, because it's less hassle and nobody has to clean up.","cn":"是的，我觉得在这边串门还是一件挺重要的事，虽然可能比过去少了一点。对好朋友和亲戚来说特别正常——你直接就上门，喝喝茶，留下来吃饭。我妈妈的朋友几乎每个周末都来。但对普通朋友或同事，大家越来越倾向于在饭店或咖啡馆见面，因为省事，谁也不用收拾屋子。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":1,"en":"Usually a lot of chatting and eating, that's the core of it. The host will serve tea first, then fruit and snacks, and if you stay long enough, a proper meal. Guests often bring a little gift and take their shoes off at the door. My grandma would be offended if a guest left without eating. There's some polite back-and-forth too, like 'don't trouble yourself', which is part of the culture.","cn":"通常就是大量的聊天和吃东西，这是核心。主人会先上茶，然后是水果和点心，如果待得够久，还有一顿正经的饭。客人一般会带点小礼物，进门要脱鞋。要是有客人什么都不吃就走，我奶奶会觉得被冒犯了。还有一些客气的推让，比如'别麻烦了'，这也是文化的一部分。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":2,"en":"I think most people here value convenience and space, but it depends on age. Younger families want a modern flat near work and schools, with good transport and shops downstairs -- they don't mind it being small. Older people often prefer a house with a garden in a quieter area, even if it's further out. And honestly, many people want a bigger place, it's just the price that stops them.","cn":"我觉得这边大多数人看重方便和空间，但这也分年龄。年轻家庭想要离上班和学校近的现代公寓，交通方便，楼下有商店——小一点他们也不介意。老人常常更喜欢安静地带有院子的房子，哪怕远一点。而且说实话，很多人都想要更大的房子，只是价格拦住了他们。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":3,"en":"The biggest difference is space, honestly. City homes are usually small flats stacked in tall buildings, but you're close to everything -- jobs, hospitals, restaurants. Countryside homes are bigger, often with a yard and fresh air, and life is slower and cheaper. But they can be far from work, so young people often leave. My aunt keeps a big country house but rents a tiny city flat for work.","cn":"说实话，最大的差别是空间。城里的房子通常是高楼里的小公寓，但你离什么都近——工作、医院、餐厅。乡下的房子更大，常有院子，空气新鲜，生活更慢也更便宜。但它们可能离工作远，所以年轻人常常离开。我姑姑留着乡下的大房子，却为工作在城里租了个小公寓。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":4,"en":"It depends on who you're visiting, I think. For close family, food is the classic gift -- fruit, a box of good tea, or some nice cakes, and it won't go to waste. For a friend's new home, people might bring a plant. For a formal visit, wine or health products are safe choices. What you avoid is anything too personal or the wrong number, like clocks.","cn":"我觉得要看你去看谁。对至亲，吃的就是经典礼物——水果、一盒好茶、或者不错的点心，反正不会浪费。去朋友新家，人们可能会带盆绿植。正式拜访的话，酒或者保健品是稳妥的选择。要避免的是太私人或者数量不对的东西，比如钟表。"},{"b":"04","c":7,"i":5,"en":"Honestly, less than I'd like. With work and everything, I probably see my closest relatives once a month or so, usually at a family dinner. A couple of friends I meet up with every few weeks for coffee. But there are plenty of relatives I only really see at weddings or during Spring Festival. I think that's pretty typical these days -- everyone's busy, and it takes real effort to keep visiting regularly.","cn":"说实话，比我希望的少。因为工作什么的，我大概一个月见最亲的亲戚一次，通常是在家庭聚餐上。有几个朋友我每几周会约一次喝咖啡。但有不少亲戚，我基本只在婚礼上或者春节期间才见得到。我觉得这在现在挺典型的——大家都忙，要定期走动还真得花点心思。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":0,"en":"I think there are several reasons. Money is the obvious one -- flights and hotels abroad can cost a fortune, and for a family it's just not realistic. Others are nervous about language barriers, or they worry about safety in unfamiliar places. My parents have never travelled abroad; they say they'd rather explore their own country, which is huge and has plenty to see. And some people simply don't enjoy the hassle of visas and airports.","cn":"我觉得有几个原因。钱是最明显的——出国的机票酒店能花掉一大笔钱，对一家人来说根本不现实。还有些人担心语言障碍，或者担心陌生地方的安全。我爸妈从没出过国；他们说宁愿好好看看自己的国家，本来地方就大，能看的也多。还有些人就是不喜欢签证、机场那些麻烦事。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":1,"en":"I'd say yes, it can be really valuable, though it's not for everyone. Taking a break between school and work gives you time to figure out what you actually want instead of just following the crowd. My cousin took a year off to travel and volunteer, and came back more focused and confident. The risk is some people drift and can't get back. So it works if you use the time well.","cn":"我觉得是的，它可能真的很有价值，虽然不适合所有人。在学业和工作之间休息一段时间，能让你想清楚自己到底想要什么，而不是随大流。我表姐休学一年去旅行、做志愿者，回来之后更专注、也更自信了。风险是有些人会变得散漫，回不来。所以只要你把时间用好了，就有用。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":2,"en":"I think the simplest fix is to change what you are doing, not just how long you do it. Going for a walk, calling a friend, or starting something with your hands usually works better than trying to force yourself to sit still. Boredom is often a sign that your brain wants something new, not more rest.","cn":"我觉得最简单的办法是改变你在做的事，而不只是坐多久。出去走走、给朋友打个电话，或者动手做点什么，往往比逼自己干坐着有用。无聊常常是大脑想要点新鲜东西的信号，不是想要更多休息。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":3,"en":"A class feels boring when the only thing happening is the teacher talking. If students never get to ask, discuss or try things themselves, it is hard to stay awake. The solution is not more entertainment but more involvement — a question, a task, or a real example that shows why the topic matters.","cn":"一堂课之所以无聊，是因为全程只有老师一个人在讲。如果学生永远没机会提问、讨论或自己尝试，就很难不打瞌睡。解决办法不是加更多娱乐，而是更多参与——一个问题、一个任务，或者一个说明这主题为什么重要的真实例子。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":4,"en":"I think it is usually a gap in interests, not a sign of disrespect. Young people grow up with fast content, so a slow story about the past can feel like a different world. But once an older person links their experience to something the young person cares about — work, love or money — the chat usually comes alive.","cn":"我觉得这通常是兴趣上的差距，而不是不尊重。年轻人是在快节奏内容里长大的，所以一段慢悠悠讲过去的故事会像另一个世界。但当长辈把自己的经历和年轻人关心的事联系起来——工作、感情或钱——聊天通常就活起来了。"},{"b":"04","c":8,"i":5,"en":"Honestly, no, I would not say so. Celebrity news is quick to take in and gives people something light to talk about, which is exactly why it spreads. But it is only boring when it is everywhere and you cannot escape it. For the people who follow it, it is genuinely entertaining.","cn":"老实说，我不会这么讲。名人新闻读起来很快，还给人提供轻松的话题，这正是它能传播的原因。只有当它铺天盖地、躲都躲不掉时它才让人烦。对真正关注它的人来说，它就是娱乐。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":0,"en":"Yes, they're everywhere in the big cities. In places like Shanghai or Shenzhen, you can barely walk a block without seeing a skyscraper, and the skyline keeps changing because they build so fast. But it's really a city thing -- go to smaller towns or the countryside and houses are low, a few floors at most. So I'd say we have plenty of tall buildings, but they're concentrated in the major urban areas.","cn":"是的，大城市里到处都是。像上海、深圳这种地方，走一个街区就能看到一栋摩天大楼，而且天际线一直在变，因为建得太快了。但这真的是城市现象——去小城镇或者乡下，房子都很矮，最多几层。所以我觉得我们有挺多高楼，但它们都集中在主要城市区域。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":1,"en":"I think the differences are mainly about purpose and style. Some are offices -- pure glass and steel, all business. Others are residential towers with hundreds of flats stacked up. And the older ones built decades ago look quite plain, while the newer ones try to be striking, with curves or unusual shapes to stand out. My city has a tower that's basically a giant landmark with a viewing deck, purely to draw tourists.","cn":"我觉得差别主要在于用途和风格。有些是写字楼——纯粹的玻璃和钢铁，一股商务感。另一些是住宅楼，一层层堆着几百套公寓。而几十年前建的老楼看起来比较朴素，新建的则想做得很抢眼，用曲线或者奇特造型来出挑。我们城市有栋楼基本就是个巨大的地标，上面有观景台，纯粹为了吸引游客。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":2,"en":"A lot of it comes down to history and geography, I'd say. Old towns grew up around a river or a market, so the streets are narrow and winding, while newer areas are planned on a grid because they start from scratch. Climate matters too -- snowy places need different roofs and spacing. And then there's money: a wealthy district invests in parks and wide roads, a poorer one makes do.","cn":"我觉得很大程度上跟历史和地理有关。老城区是围绕一条河或者一个集市发展起来的，所以街道又窄又弯；而新城区是按网格规划的，因为可以白手起家。气候也有影响——下大雪的地方屋顶和间距都不一样。然后还有钱：富裕的区会投资公园和宽阔的马路，穷一点的就只能将就。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":3,"en":"There are real benefits, I think. You get amazing views, especially if you're high up -- my friend lives on the 30th floor and her sunsets are unreal. You're also usually close to shops, transport and work, so you save time and don't need a car. And in crowded cities, building up is more efficient than spreading out. The downside is lifts break down and you can feel disconnected from neighbours.","cn":"我觉得确实有实实在在的好处。你能看到很棒的景色，尤其是住得高的话——我朋友住在30楼，她那儿的日落简直不真实。你通常还离商店、交通和工作都近，省时间，也不用买车。而且在拥挤的城市里，往上建比往外摊更高效。缺点是电梯会坏，而且可能跟邻居有点疏离。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":4,"en":"I think it's half creative, half practical. Some people just enjoy it -- it's a hobby, like painting, and you get to put your own stamp on the place. My aunt redid her whole living room herself and was so proud of it. But for most, it's about cost, because hiring a designer is expensive. And there's the joy of a space that's truly yours, not a copy of every other flat.","cn":"我觉得一半是创作，一半是务实。有些人就是喜欢——这是一种爱好，像画画一样，而且你能给这个地方打上自己的印记。我姑姑自己把整个客厅翻新了，特别自豪。但对大多数人来说，是为了省钱，因为请设计师很贵。还有就是那种拥有一个真正属于自己的空间的快乐，而不是每套公寓的翻版。"},{"b":"04","c":9,"i":5,"en":"From what I see, most people here like something clean and simple -- light colours, wooden floors, not too much clutter. It's practical and it makes small flats feel bigger, which matters when space is tight. Young people might add a bit of personality, like plants or a feature wall, but nothing too wild. I think the trend is away from the heavy, dark furniture my grandparents had, towards a bright, airy, minimalist look.","cn":"据我观察，这边大多数人喜欢干净简单的风格——浅色、木地板、不要太乱。这样实用，而且能让小公寓显得更大，在空间紧张的时候很重要。年轻人可能会加点个性，比如绿植或者一面背景墙，但也不会太夸张。我觉得趋势是从我祖父母那代厚重的深色家具，转向明亮、通透、极简的风格。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":0,"en":"I don't think it's really the subject's fault -- it's usually the way it's taught. Kids get bored when they're just made to memorise dates and facts for an exam, with no stories or discussion. My history class was like that until a new teacher started showing us films and asking what we'd have done. Suddenly everyone paid attention. So education isn't boring; dull teaching is. Give kids some choice and relevance and they light up.","cn":"我不觉得这真是学科的错——通常问题出在教的方式。当孩子只是为了考试被逼着背年份和事实，没有故事也没有讨论，当然会无聊。我的历史课本来是那样，直到来了个新老师，给我们放电影，还问我们如果换作是你会怎么做。一下子所有人都专注了。所以教育本身不无聊，无聊的是教学。给孩子一点选择、一点相关性，他们就活过来了。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":1,"en":"I think it's partly because the world has changed so fast. Older people grew up in a very different time, so their advice feels out of date -- like telling kids who grew up with phones to just go outside more. Also, young people want to be treated as equals, and some older people talk down to them. My grandpa lectures me non-stop, and even when he's right, I switch off because of the tone.","cn":"我觉得一部分是因为世界变化太快了。老一辈成长的环境完全不同，所以他们的建议显得过时——比如叫从小玩手机长大的孩子'多出去走走'。还有，年轻人希望被平等对待，而有些老人会居高临下地跟他们说话。我爷爷没完没了地教训我，就算他说得对，我也会因为那种口气而听不进去。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":2,"en":"Honestly, there's no shortage of things. The simplest is to just move -- go for a walk or hit the gym, because sitting around makes boredom worse. Others dive into a game or a show to escape for a while. I usually call a friend or cook something, since doing something with my hands resets my mood. The trick is to actually do something, not scroll aimlessly.","cn":"说实话，能做的事有的是。最简单的就是动起来——出去走走或者去健身房，因为干坐着只会更无聊。也有人打游戏或者追剧来逃避一会儿。我一般会打个电话给朋友，或者做点吃的，因为动手做点事能重置我的心情。诀窍就是真的去做点什么，而不是没头没脑地刷手机。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":3,"en":"Usually it's because the class is one-way: the teacher talks, students just listen and copy. If there's no interaction or hands-on stuff, eyes glaze over fast. Another reason is the material is too far removed from students' lives. My chemistry teacher turned formulas into everyday examples -- why soap works, that sort of thing -- and it was brilliant. The fix: make it interactive, relevant and slower when people are lost.","cn":"通常是因为课堂是单向的：老师讲，学生只是听和抄。如果没有互动或者动手的东西，很快就眼神发直了。另一个原因是内容离学生的生活太远。我的化学老师把公式变成日常的例子——肥皂为什么能去污之类的——就特别棒。解决办法就是：让它互动、有相关性，人家听不懂时就放慢速度。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":4,"en":"A few things, I'd say. Their life experiences are just so different that there's not much to connect on -- my grandma talks about her village and people my age have never lived like that. The pace is different too; older people often talk slowly and repeat themselves, which young people find hard to sit through. And there's a respect gap, where the young feel they can't disagree or joke.","cn":"我觉得有几个原因。他们的生活经历太不一样了，没什么能共鸣的地方——我奶奶聊她那个村子的往事，而我这个年纪的人从没那样生活过。节奏也不同；老人经常说得慢，还重复，年轻人很难坐得住。还有一点尊卑上的隔阂，年轻人觉得自己不能反驳或者开玩笑。"},{"b":"04","c":10,"i":5,"en":"Honestly, it depends on the person, but I'd say yes for most. Celebrity gossip is the same few stories -- who's dating who, who wore what -- and after a while it all blurs together. My parents read it for light entertainment, like junk food for the brain, but nobody takes it seriously. That said, some celebrity news matters, like a famous athlete doing real charity work. But the daily stuff is pretty empty.","cn":"说实话，这要看人，但我觉得对大多数人来说是的。明星八卦就是那几件事——谁跟谁在一起了、谁穿了什么——过一阵子全都混在一起分不清了。我父母把它当轻松的消遣来读，就像给大脑吃的垃圾食品，但没人当真。话虽如此，有些明星新闻是有意义的，比如某个著名运动员做真正的慈善。但日常那些内容还是挺空的。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":0,"en":"I think it's a mixed bag. On the plus side, ads keep us informed -- without them I'd never discover new products or good deals. They also fund free content, like videos and apps we don't pay for. But the downside is they push us to buy things we don't need, and they're everywhere, so it's hard to escape. So they're useful, but the constant pressure to spend is annoying.","cn":"我觉得这是好坏参半。好的一面是，广告让我们获取信息——没有它们我根本不会发现新东西或者好优惠。它们还养活了免费内容，比如我们不用付费的视频和App。但缺点在于它们推着我们买不需要的东西，而且无处不在，很难躲开。所以它们有用，但那种不断催你花钱的压力很烦人。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":1,"en":"Because celebrities already have trust and attention, so a company can borrow that instantly. If a famous athlete says a drink is good, fans believe it far more than some random actor nobody knows. It's effective for big launches but not always reliable -- I once bought a shampoo a singer promoted, and it was rubbish. So they help get noticed, but people are getting smarter and know stars are paid to say nice things.","cn":"因为名人本身就带着信任和关注度，公司可以直接借用。如果一个著名运动员说某种饮料好，粉丝信他，远胜过信一个谁也不认识的路人演员。这对大型新品发布很有效，但也不总靠谱——我有次买了个歌手代言的洗发水，结果很烂。所以名人能帮产品被注意到，但大家越来越精明，知道明星是收了钱才说好话的。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":2,"en":"For me, it's got to be honesty and clarity. If an ad confuses you or feels fake, you just skip it -- I close anything that looks suspicious in two seconds. A good ad tells you what the product does and why you'd want it, simply. After that, a bit of creativity or emotion helps it stick. So it's truth first, then a clever idea, then a splash of feeling.","cn":"对我来说，一定是诚实和清晰。如果一条广告让你看不懂，或者感觉很假，你直接就跳过了——看起来可疑的东西我两秒就关掉。好的广告会简单地告诉你这个产品是干什么的、你为什么想要它。在这之后，一点创意或者情感能帮它被记住。所以首先是真实，然后是巧妙的点子，再撒上一点情感。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":3,"en":"Usually because they're lazy and generic. If an ad just shows the product and a price with some plain music, there's nothing to hook you -- you've seen it a hundred times. Repetition is another killer; seeing the same thing ten times a day makes people cringe. And some are just confusing, so you don't even know what's being sold. So dull, repetitive or unclear ads are the boring ones.","cn":"通常是因为做得懒、又千篇一律。如果一条广告只是展示产品加个价格配点平淡的音乐，就没东西能勾住你——这种东西你见过一百遍了。重复也是杀手；同一条一天看十遍就让人想吐。还有些纯粹是看不懂，你都不知道卖的是什么。所以单调、重复或者含糊的广告就是最无聊的。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I'd argue it's essential, especially in a crowded market. If nobody knows your product exists, it doesn't matter how good it is -- you'll sell nothing. Advertising builds awareness and trust; a clever campaign can turn a small brand into a household name almost overnight. My friend's bakery grew just from posting nicely shot photos online. That said, it has to match the product, or it backfires.","cn":"是的，我甚至觉得这是必需的，尤其是在这么挤的市场里。如果没人知道你的产品存在，再好也没用——你什么也卖不出去。广告建立认知和信任；一次巧妙的营销能让一个小品牌几乎一夜之间变得家喻户晓。我朋友的面包店就是靠在网上发拍得好看的照片火起来的。话虽如此，它得跟产品匹配，否则会反噬。"},{"b":"04","c":11,"i":5,"en":"I think they do different jobs, so it depends on the goal. Online is more targeted and measurable -- it can put your ad right in front of people who already like similar things, and it's cheaper. That's why so many brands flood social media. But offline, like billboards or TV, can feel more trustworthy and reach older people who aren't online much. So ideally you'd use both.","cn":"我觉得它们干的是不同的活，所以要看目标。线上更精准、更好衡量——它能把广告直接推给已经喜欢类似东西的人，而且更便宜。这就是为什么这么多品牌扎堆社交媒体。但线下，比如户外广告牌或者电视，会显得更可信，还能触达不太上网的老人。所以理想情况下两个都用。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I think they should, but in a hands-on way, not just from a textbook. Kids should learn to treat animals with respect and understand that they're living things with feelings, not toys. When I was little we had a rabbit in the classroom, and taking care of it taught us more about responsibility than any lesson. It's also a good way to start talking about the environment.","cn":"是的，我觉得应该，但要动手去做，而不是光看课本。孩子应该学会尊重动物、明白它们是活生生、有感情的，不是玩具。我小时候班里有只兔子，照顾它教给我们的责任感比任何课都多。这也是一个很好的切入点，开始聊环境。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":1,"en":"I can see both sides here. In a tiny flat, a big dog would be miserable and the neighbours too, so maybe limits make sense for certain animals. But a blanket ban is too harsh -- a goldfish or a cat can be perfectly happy in an apartment and brings a lot of joy. My neighbour lives in a small flat with two cats and they're thriving. So I'd regulate it sensibly rather than ban pets.","cn":"我两方面都能理解。在一间很小的公寓里，大型犬会很痛苦，邻居也受不了，所以对某些动物设限制也许有道理。但一刀切地禁止就太苛刻了——一条金鱼或一只猫在公寓里完全可以很快乐，还能带来很多欢乐。我邻居住小公寓养了两只猫，它们活得很好。所以我觉得应该合理管理，而不是禁止养宠物。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":2,"en":"I completely agree, and honestly it's the same in my home. Our dog is definitely part of the family -- my mum talks to him like a person and he sleeps on the sofa. For a lot of people, especially those living alone or the elderly, a pet is a companion who never judges them, which is priceless. That said, treating them as family also means not abandoning them when it gets inconvenient.","cn":"我完全同意，说实话我家也是这样。我们的狗绝对是家庭一员——我妈跟他说话就像跟人一样，他还睡沙发。对很多人，尤其是独居的人或老人来说，宠物是个从不评判他们的伙伴，这很珍贵。话虽如此，把它们当家人也意味着不能嫌麻烦就抛弃它们。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":3,"en":"Yes, keeping pets is really common here, especially dogs and cats, though the trend has changed a bit. In the past people mostly had dogs for guarding the house, but now lots of young urban couples get a cat or a small dog purely as a companion. You see pet strollers and pet cafes everywhere now. So it's definitely popular, and the pet industry is booming because of it.","cn":"是的，这边养宠物非常普遍，尤其是狗和猫，不过趋势有点变化。以前人们养狗大多是为了看家，而现在很多年轻的城市情侣养猫或小狗纯粹是为了陪伴。现在到处都能看到宠物推车和宠物咖啡馆。所以它肯定很流行，宠物行业也因此很火。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":4,"en":"There are quite a few, I'd say. The biggest is companionship -- a pet greets you when you come home and makes an empty flat feel warm, which is huge if you live alone. They also get you moving; my friend walks her dog twice a day and it keeps her fit. And for kids, a pet teaches responsibility and empathy. The downside is the cost and the mess, of course.","cn":"我觉得好处挺多的。最大的是陪伴——宠物会在你回家时迎接你，让空荡荡的屋子变得温暖，如果你独居的话这太重要了。它们还能让你动起来；我朋友每天遛两次狗，身体一直很好。对孩子来说，宠物能教会责任感和同理心。当然，缺点就是花费和收拾的问题。"},{"b":"04","c":12,"i":5,"en":"I think it's because animals make the story instantly appealing and easy to understand. A talking fox or a brave little rabbit grabs a child's attention far more than a human character would, and kids remember the lesson better. There's also a long tradition -- fables have used animals for centuries to teach morals gently. So hiding a lesson in an animal story is a clever way to guide children.","cn":"我觉得是因为动物能让故事立刻变得有吸引力、又好懂。一只会说话的狐狸或者一只勇敢的小兔子，比人类角色更能抓住孩子的注意力，孩子也更容易记住那个道理。还有悠久的传统——寓言几百年来都用动物温和地讲道理。所以把道理藏在一个动物故事里，是引导孩子的聪明办法。"},{"b":"04","c":13,"i":0,"en":"Absolutely, they're vital. Rivers provide drinking water, irrigate farmland and carry goods, so entire civilisations grew up along them -- the big rivers here have fed millions for centuries. Lakes are just as important: they store water, support fishing and act like a natural air conditioner for nearby cities. And of course they're beautiful, drawing tourists. So yes, I'd say rivers and lakes are like the veins of a country, keeping everything alive.","cn":"绝对重要，它们是命脉。河流提供饮用水、灌溉农田、运输货物，所以整片文明都是沿着河流发展起来的——这边的大河几百年来养活了数百万人。湖泊同样重要：它们蓄水、支持渔业，还像附近城市的天然空调。当然它们还很美，吸引游客。所以是的，我觉得江河湖泊就像国家的血管，让一切都活着。"},{"b":"04","c":13,"i":1,"en":"Swimming is the big one, of course, and in the summer the beaches and lakes get packed. Rowing and dragon boat racing are quite popular too -- my city holds a dragon boat festival every year and the whole river is lined with crowds. In coastal areas, surfing and sailing have fans, though they're a bit niche. So most water sports here are casual, like a family day at the lake.","cn":"游泳当然是最大的一项，到了夏天海滩和湖边都挤满了人。划船和龙舟赛也挺流行——我们城市每年都办龙舟节，整条河边都站满了人。在沿海地区，冲浪和帆船也有人玩，不过还算比较小众。所以这边大部分水上运动都是休闲性质的，比如一家人去湖边玩一天。"},{"b":"04","c":13,"i":2,"en":"Yes, historically very much so -- rivers were the original highways before roads and railways, and boats could carry heavy cargo cheaply. They're still used today for bulk goods like coal, sand and grain, which is much cheaper than trucks. My grandfather worked on a cargo boat, so I've heard plenty about it. The catch is speed -- river transport is slow, so for anything urgent people use roads.","cn":"是的，历史上非常有用——在有公路和铁路之前，河流就是最早的高速路，船能便宜地运很重的货。今天它们还用来运煤、沙子、粮食这类散货，比卡车便宜得多。我爷爷在货船上干过，所以我听过不少。麻烦的是速度——水运慢，所以急的东西人们还是走公路。"},{"b":"04","c":13,"i":3,"en":"They're a huge draw, I'd say. A beautiful lake or a famous river becomes the centrepiece of a region's tourism -- boat trips, waterfront cafes, the whole thing. My hometown has a river that people come from far away to see, and the hotels and restaurants along it all depend on those visitors. So it genuinely boosts tourism, as long as the water stays clean and they don't overbuild.","cn":"我觉得它们的吸引力巨大。一个漂亮的湖或者一条有名的河，会成为一个地区旅游的核心——游船、滨水的咖啡馆，全套都有。我老家有条河，人们从很远的地方来看，沿岸的酒店和餐厅都靠这些游客。所以它确实能带动旅游，前提是水质保持干净、也别过度开发。"},{"b":"04","c":14,"i":0,"en":"Oh, quite a lot. The most obvious is uniform -- nearly every school here has one, so everyone looks the same. Then there are rules about arriving on time, no phones during class, and finishing homework. Some schools are stricter and ban long hair or makeup. My old school even had a rule about which shoes you could wear. So basically they cover everything from what you wear to how you behave, and they're pretty detailed.","cn":"哦，挺多的。最明显的校服——这边几乎所有学校都有校服，大家穿得一样。然后还有关于准时到、上课不能用手机、完成作业的规矩。有些学校更严格，不许留长发或者化妆。我以前的学校甚至有规定你能穿哪种鞋。所以基本上从你穿什么到你怎么表现，都有规矩，而且规定得挺细。"},{"b":"04","c":14,"i":1,"en":"Yes, I think they matter, but there's a line. Good rules create a safe, fair environment -- without them, classes would be chaos and quieter kids would suffer. They also teach discipline and respect, which you need later at work. But rules that are pointless or unfair just breed resentment and make kids sneaky. My cousin's school banned talking at lunch, which everyone thought was ridiculous and ignored anyway. So sensible rules, yes; petty ones, no.","cn":"是的，我觉得重要，但有个度。好的规矩能营造安全、公平的环境——没有规矩，课堂就乱套，安静的孩子会吃亏。它们还能教会纪律和尊重，这些以后工作也用得上。但那些没用或者不公平的规定只会滋生不满，还让孩子学会阳奉阴违。我表弟的学校不许午饭时间讲话，大家都觉得很荒唐，反正也没人遵守。所以合理的规矩，可以；鸡毛蒜皮的，不行。"},{"b":"04","c":14,"i":2,"en":"Some are, definitely. Especially the ones that feel like they're just about control -- too many restrictions on hair, clothes or where you can sit. Kids will moan about anything, but when the complaint is widespread, it usually means the rule's out of touch. That said, a lot of students accept the useful rules, like no phones or no cheating, because they see the point. So it's the petty rules that really annoy them.","cn":"有些肯定是。尤其是那些感觉只是为了控制的规定——对发型、穿着、或者你能坐哪儿管得太多。孩子什么都会抱怨，但当抱怨很普遍的时候，通常说明这条规定脱离实际了。话虽如此，很多学生其实接受那些有用的规定，比如不许用手机、不许作弊，因为他们看得到意义。所以真正让他们烦的是那些琐碎的规定。"},{"b":"04","c":14,"i":3,"en":"At work the main ones are punctuality, keeping information confidential, and treating colleagues and clients with respect. Dressing appropriately matters in many jobs too. Then there are safety rules -- my friend works in a factory and the no-jewellery, no-loose-clothing rules are there to protect people, not annoy them. And of course you shouldn't use company time or resources for personal stuff. So basically the rules keep things efficient, safe and professional.","cn":"工作场所主要的规矩是准时、保守信息机密、尊重同事和客户。很多工作上着装得体也很重要。然后还有安全规定——我朋友在工厂上班，不许戴首饰、不许穿宽松衣服这些规定是为了保护人，不是来烦人的。当然你也不该拿公司的时间或资源做私事。所以基本上这些规矩是为了让事情高效、安全、专业。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":0,"en":"I think it gives them direction. Without a dream to aim at, kids can drift and just follow whatever everyone else does. Having an ambition, even a silly one that changes later, teaches them to work towards something and to deal with setbacks. My nephew wanted to be a pilot, and just chasing that made him study maths harder. So let the ambition be theirs, and let it change.","cn":"我觉得这能给孩子一个方向。没有梦想去追，孩子就容易飘着，只是跟着别人走。有个目标，哪怕是个以后会变的傻梦想，也能教他们为一件事努力、去面对挫折。我侄子想当飞行员，光是为了追这个，他数学都学得更用功了。所以让这个目标是他们自己的，也允许它改变。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":1,"en":"I'd say it's fine as long as they don't trample on people to get there. Chasing a high position shows drive and responsibility, and honestly we need people who want to lead -- someone has to run things. My old boss came up from nothing and worked incredibly hard for her role, and she deserved it. The problem is when it turns into pure ego. So it's okay if you keep your values.","cn":"我觉得只要不踩着别人往上爬就行。追求高位说明有干劲、有责任心，说实话我们需要想当领导的人——总得有人管事。我以前的老板是从一无所有做上来的，为这个位置拼了命地努力，她当之无愧。问题在于当它变成纯粹的虚荣。所以只要守住自己的价值观就没问题。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":2,"en":"I think it's mostly about finding a sense of belonging. When you're young and unsure of yourself, a superstar seems perfect and confident, so being a fan gives you an identity and a community of people who love the same thing. My younger cousin is obsessed with a K-pop group, and the fandom gave her real friends. It's only unhealthy if it takes over everything.","cn":"我觉得主要是在找一种归属感。当你年轻、对自己没把握的时候，一个巨星看起来完美又自信，所以当粉丝能给你一个身份、一群喜欢同样东西的人。我表妹特别迷一个韩团，那个粉丝圈子让她交到了真朋友。只有当它占据一切的时候才变得不健康。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":3,"en":"On balance, yes, I'd say it's good -- if it's the healthy kind. Ambition pushes you to improve, to set goals and actually chase them, and people like that tend to achieve more and feel more satisfied. My friend's drive to open her own studio kept her learning for years. The flip side is it can make people stressed or ruthless. So ambition's a strength, as long as you don't sacrifice your health for it.","cn":"总的来说，是的，我觉得是好事——只要是健康的那种。野心会推着你进步，去定目标、真的去追，这样的人往往成就更多、也更有满足感。我朋友想开自己工作室的这股劲，让她学了好多年。反面是它会让人压力大、变得不择手段。所以野心是一种优点，只要你不是为了它牺牲健康。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":4,"en":"Yes, in a sense, though it should be about the team's goals, not just personal glory. A team works best when everyone's ambitious for the group -- wanting to beat the target, take pride in doing good work. Without that drive, people coast and nothing gets done. But you also need people willing to support others. My last project succeeded because two ambitious people pushed hard and the rest kept up.","cn":"是的，某种意义上需要，不过应该是为了团队的目标，而不只是个人风光。当每个人都为集体有野心的时候，团队才运转得最好——想超越目标、为做好工作而自豪。没有这股劲，大家就混日子，什么都干不成。但你也需要愿意支持别人的人。我上一个项目之所以成功，是因为两个有野心的人使劲往前推，其他人也跟着跟上来了。"},{"b":"04","c":15,"i":5,"en":"Yes, I think they should, within reason. If a child has a real passion, parents backing it -- with money, time or just encouragement -- makes a huge difference, and a lack of support can crush a dream. My friend wanted to study art and her parents funded it, and she's now doing well. But support doesn't mean blind approval; they should give honest advice about the risks too.","cn":"是的，我觉得应该，在合理范围内。如果孩子有真正的热爱，父母支持它——不管是钱、时间还是仅仅鼓励——差别会很大，而缺少支持可能会毁掉一个梦想。我朋友想学美术，她父母出钱支持，现在她发展得挺好。但支持不等于盲目赞同；他们也应该就风险给出诚实的建议。"},{"b":"04","c":16,"i":0,"en":"Honestly, fewer and fewer, especially young people. My parents still get a paper delivered, but I get all my news on my phone -- it's free and instant. The printed paper is mainly popular with older readers who like the ritual of it over breakfast. That said, a lot of people still read the news, just not on paper. So it's the format that's dying, not the interest.","cn":"说实话，看的人越来越少了，尤其是年轻人。我父母还订报纸，但我所有新闻都在手机上看——免费又即时。纸质报纸主要是年纪大的读者喜欢，他们享受早餐时读报的那个仪式感。话虽如此，很多人还是看新闻的，只是不看纸质的。所以死掉的是那个形式，而不是那份兴趣。"},{"b":"04","c":16,"i":1,"en":"I'd say it depends on the person and their stage of life. Older people tend to prefer local news -- what's happening in their neighbourhood, local prices, school stuff -- because it affects them directly. Young people often lean towards international news, especially big dramatic events or things about other cultures. I'm a bit of both; I check global headlines, but I also care if my city is building a new subway line. So it really varies.","cn":"我觉得这要看人和他们所处的阶段。老人往往更喜欢本地新闻——他们小区发生了什么、本地物价、学校的事——因为直接影响他们。年轻人常常更偏向国际新闻，尤其是重大的戏剧性事件或者关于其他文化的东西。我算是两者都有一点；我会看全球头条，但也在乎我这座城市是不是要修新地铁线。所以真的因人而异。"},{"b":"04","c":16,"i":2,"en":"I think it is important, though it's a tricky thing. A shared identity gives people a sense of belonging and unity -- it's what makes strangers feel like they're part of the same team, especially in hard times. It also helps preserve language, traditions and history. But it can go too far and turn into looking down on outsiders. So a healthy national identity is about shared culture and pride, not about feeling superior to others.","cn":"我觉得重要，不过这是个微妙的东西。一种共享的身份认同能给人归属感和凝聚力——正是它让陌生人感觉是同一队的，尤其在困难时期。它也有助于保存语言、传统和历史。但它也可能走得太远，变成看不起外人。所以健康的国民认同是关乎共同的文化和自豪感，而不是觉得自己高人一等。"},{"b":"04","c":16,"i":3,"en":"I think it mainly comes from everyday exposure and education. Learning your country's history, language and festivals at school is the base, but it's really the lived stuff that sticks -- celebrating Spring Festival with family, eating the traditional food, hearing your grandparents' stories. Media and national events like the Olympics also build pride. My nephew learned more about our culture from cooking dumplings with my mum than from any textbook.","cn":"我觉得它主要来自日常的耳濡目染和教育。在学校学自己国家的历史、语言和节日是基础，但真正扎根的是那些亲身体验——和家人一起过春节、吃传统食物、听爷爷奶奶讲故事。媒体和像奥运会这样的活动也会建立自豪感。我侄子跟我妈一起包饺子学到的文化，比从任何课本学的都多。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":0,"en":"I think it affects the law in two big ways. First, technology creates completely new situations that old laws never imagined -- things like data privacy, online fraud, or who's responsible when a self-driving car crashes. So the law has to keep catching up. Second, technology changes how the law works; now there are cameras and digital records, so it's easier to enforce and to prove what happened.","cn":"我觉得科技主要从两方面影响法律。第一，科技制造出旧法律从没想象过的全新情境——比如数据隐私、网络诈骗，或者自动驾驶汽车出事故该谁负责。所以法律得不断追赶。第二，科技改变了法律的运作方式；现在有摄像头和数字记录，执行起来更容易，也更容易证明发生了什么。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":1,"en":"Oh, quite a lot. The most obvious is uniform -- nearly every school here has one, so everyone looks the same. Then there are rules about arriving on time, no phones during class, and finishing homework. Some schools are stricter and ban long hair or makeup. My old school even had a rule about which shoes you could wear. So basically they cover everything from what you wear to how you behave, and they're pretty detailed.","cn":"哦，挺多的。最明显的校服——这边几乎所有学校都有校服，大家穿得一样。然后还有关于准时到、上课不能用手机、完成作业的规矩。有些学校更严格，不许留长发或者化妆。我以前的学校甚至有规定你能穿哪种鞋。所以基本上从你穿什么到你怎么表现，都有规矩，而且规定得挺细。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":2,"en":"I doubt we'll ever have one that absolutely everyone accepts, honestly. Countries have different cultures, religions and priorities, so something fine in one place offends another -- think of the death penalty or free speech. So a single global rulebook is unrealistic. But we can agree on some basics, like banning chemical weapons or protecting the oceans. So shared rules on big issues, yes; total agreement, no.","cn":"说实话，我怀疑我们永远不会有一个人人都绝对接受的法律。各国文化、宗教和优先级都不同，所以在一个地方没问题的事，在另一个地方就冒犯到了——想想死刑或者言论自由。所以一本全球统一的法律手册不现实。但我们能在一些基本点上达成一致，比如禁止化学武器或者保护海洋。所以大问题上有共同规则，可以；完全一致，不行。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":3,"en":"There are a fair number, especially in recent years. The big ones are laws limiting pollution from factories, rules against cutting down forests, and bans on burning things that create heavy smog. There are also rules about protecting endangered animals and, more recently, rules pushing recycling and cutting plastic waste, like the plastic bag ban in shops. My city even fines people for littering. So the framework is there, though enforcement isn't always perfect.","cn":"有不少，尤其是近几年。大的那些是限制工厂污染的法规、禁止砍伐森林的规定，还有禁止焚烧会造成严重雾霾的东西。还有保护濒危动物的规定，以及近些年推动回收、减少塑料垃圾的规定，比如商店的塑料袋禁令。我们城市甚至对乱扔垃圾的人罚款。所以框架是有了，虽然执行不一定总到位。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":4,"en":"I think there is a real risk, especially among students. When AI can answer almost anything in seconds, it is very tempting to skip the thinking part. The danger is not using it, but losing the habit of working things out yourself. Used as a helper it is fine; used as a replacement for your own brain, it weakens you.","cn":"我觉得确实有风险，尤其是在学生当中。当 AI 能在几秒内回答几乎任何问题，跳过思考那一步就非常诱人。危险不在于用它，而在于丢掉自己动脑的习惯。当工具用没问题；当自己大脑的替代品用，就会让你变弱。"},{"b":"04","c":17,"i":5,"en":"I would say the key is to design tasks AI cannot simply answer. Instead of asking for a summary, teachers can ask students to explain their own reasoning or defend a choice in class. In other words, shift the work from finding the answer to showing the thinking, because that is something a machine cannot fake.","cn":"我觉得关键在于设计出 AI 没法简单回答的任务。与其让学生写一份摘要，老师可以让学生解释自己的推理，或在课堂上为自己的选择辩护。换句话说，把重点从「找到答案」转到「展示思考过程」，因为那是机器没法造假的。"},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":0,"en":"Well, I think students should follow some basic rules, like showing up on time and respecting the teacher. But honestly, I do not think school should drown kids in rules. When I was in high school, the only rule I really remember is that we had to hand in our phones before class, and that actually made us talk to each other more.","cn":"嗯，我觉得学生应该遵守一些基本规则，比如准时到校、尊重老师。但说实话，我觉得学校不该用规则把学生淹没。我上高中时，唯一真正记得的规则就是上课前必须交手机，而那确实让我们更多地互相交流了."},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":1,"en":"I would say most people do follow the law, at least the obvious ones like not stealing or driving through red lights. But there is a lot of small rule-breaking too, things like jaywalking or copying homework. It is kind of a mixed picture, because people obey when they feel watched, but slack off when nobody is looking.","cn":"我觉得大多数人确实会遵守法律，至少是那些明显的，比如不偷窃、不闯红灯。但也有很多小的违规，比如乱穿马路或抄作业。情况挺复杂的，因为人们在感觉被盯着时会守规矩，没人看的时候就松懈了."},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":2,"en":"To me, good behaviour basically means treating other people with respect. Simple stuff, you know, queuing instead of pushing, saying thank you, keeping your voice down on the bus. My mum always told me that good manners cost nothing, and I think that is still true, whether you are a kid or an adult.","cn":"对我来说，好的行为基本就是指尊重他人。都是很简单的事，比如排队而不是插队、说谢谢、在公交上压低声音。我妈总说，礼貌不花一分钱，我觉得这话到今天都没错，不管是小孩还是大人."},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":3,"en":"Yes, definitely. I mean, a lot of what kids know about law actually comes from outside school, like police dramas on TV or their parents explaining why you cannot just take things. When I was little, my dad got a parking ticket and used it as a lesson to explain why rules exist, so real life teaches it too.","cn":"当然可以。我是说，孩子对法律的了解，很多其实来自校外，比如电视上的警匪剧，或者父母解释为什么不能随便拿别人东西。我小时候，我爸吃了张停车罚单，就拿它当教材，跟我解释为什么要有规则，所以现实生活也会教这些."},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":4,"en":"The biggest benefit is that rules keep things fair and safe. If everyone can drive however they want, the roads would be chaos. I also think following rules builds trust, because you know what to expect from others. It can feel a bit annoying sometimes, but in the long run it makes daily life smoother for everyone.","cn":"最大的好处是规则让事情公平又安全。如果每个人都随意开车，路上就乱套了。我还觉得遵守规则能建立信任，因为你知道别人会怎么做。有时候会觉得有点烦，但长远来看，它让每个人的日常生活都更顺畅."},{"b":"04","c":18,"i":5,"en":"I think the best way is to model it rather than lecture. Kids copy what they see, so if parents queue, recycle and admit mistakes, the child will pick that up. Also explaining the reason behind a rule helps a lot. When my mum told me why we buckle up, I actually remembered it instead of just obeying blindly.","cn":"我觉得最好的办法是言传身教，而不是说教。孩子会模仿他们看到的东西，所以如果父母排队、垃圾分类、承认错误，孩子就会学到。另外解释规则背后的原因也很有用。我妈告诉我为什么要系安全带后，我就真的记住了，而不是盲从."},{"b":"04","c":19,"i":0,"en":"I think in my country short videos are huge right now, the kind you scroll through on your phone for hours. People also love cooking clips and funny pet videos. My cousin literally watches thirty-minute video essays about movies, while my grandma only watches dance videos, so it really depends on the age group.","cn":"我觉得在我们国家，短视频现在特别火，就是那种能在手机上刷好几个小时的。人们也很爱看做饭视频和搞笑的宠物视频。我表弟会看那种讲电影的三十分钟长视频，而我奶奶只看跳舞视频，所以真的很看年龄."},{"b":"04","c":19,"i":1,"en":"It depends what you want. For practical skills, like fixing a bike or doing a yoga move, videos are way better because you can actually see it. But for deep thinking, I would still pick books. I mean, I learned to cook from videos in a week, but I never really understand a topic unless I read about it slowly.","cn":"这要看你想要什么。对于实用技能，比如修自行车或做瑜伽动作，视频好得多，因为你能亲眼看到。但如果是深入思考，我还是会选书。我是说，我用视频一周就学会了做饭，但一个话题如果不慢慢看书，我就没法真正理解."},{"b":"04","c":19,"i":2,"en":"Loads of things, honestly. My sister learned to do her own make-up from tutorials, and my friend picked up basic guitar chords the same way. Beyond that, people learn coding, baking, even interview skills. I think the key is that videos let you pause and replay, so you can practise until you get the hang of it.","cn":"说实话，能学的东西太多了。我姐姐是从教学视频里学会自己化妆的，我朋友也是用同样的方式学会了基本的吉他和弦。除此之外，人们还学编程、烘焙，甚至面试技巧。我觉得关键在于视频能暂停和重播，所以你可以一直练到上手为止."},{"b":"04","c":19,"i":3,"en":"I think a video goes viral when it hits some emotion really fast, like making you laugh or feel angry in the first three seconds. Timing matters too, like a clip that fits a current trend. My friend posted a silly video of his cat and it blew up overnight, mostly because it was short and genuinely funny.","cn":"我觉得一个视频之所以会爆火，是它能在极短时间内击中某种情绪，比如前三秒就让你笑或让你生气。时机也很重要，比如一个契合当下热点的片段。我朋友发了一段他家猫的傻视频，一夜之间就火了，主要是因为它短而且确实好笑."},{"b":"04","c":19,"i":4,"en":"I think it is mostly young people. They change jobs, cities and habits more often, partly because they are still searching for what fits. Older people usually have a routine built around work and family, so changing it costs more. That said, once someone retires, they often change again, just in a different direction.","cn":"我觉得主要是年轻人。他们更常换工作、换城市、换习惯，部分原因是他们还在寻找什么适合自己。年长的人通常有一套围绕工作和家庭建立起来的作息，改起来代价更大。不过一旦退休，他们往往又会改变，只是方向不同。"},{"b":"04","c":20,"i":0,"en":"I would say the hardest part is actually speaking without fear of making mistakes. People freeze up because they are scared of sounding silly. Also, finding real chances to practise is tough, you know, if nobody around you speaks that language. I found memorising words easy, but holding a real conversation was a different story.","cn":"我觉得最难的部分其实是开口说而不怕犯错。人们会因为怕听起来很傻而卡住。另外，找到真正练习的机会也很难，比如如果你周围没人说那门语言。我发现背单词容易，但真的进行一场对话就完全是另一回事了."},{"b":"04","c":20,"i":1,"en":"Yes, I think it matters a lot. For one thing, it opens up jobs, like my aunt got a better position just because she speaks decent English. But beyond work, it lets you connect with people and understand other cultures. It is also just fun to order food in a foreign country and actually be understood.","cn":"是的，我觉得这很重要。一方面，它能打开工作机会，比如我姑姑就因为英语还不错而升了职。但除了工作，它还能让你和人建立联系、理解别的文化。而且在国外点餐时真的被人听懂，本身就很有趣."},{"b":"04","c":20,"i":2,"en":"I would say a mix works best, but if I had to choose, groups are more motivating. When I studied French alone, I gave up after two weeks. In a class, though, other people kept me going, and we could practise with each other. That said, studying alone is better for grammar drills, so it really depends on the goal.","cn":"我觉得两者结合最好，但如果非要选，我选小组，因为更有动力。我一个人学法语时，两周就放弃了。但在班里，别人会让我坚持下去，我们还能互相练习。话说回来，独自学习更适合练语法，所以真的要看目标."},{"b":"04","c":20,"i":3,"en":"Honestly, the best way is just to use it as much as possible, even badly. Watching shows without subtitles helped me a lot, and forcing myself to chat with strangers online was scary but effective. You have to accept sounding clumsy at first, because that is just part of the process of getting fluent.","cn":"说实话，最好的办法就是尽量多用，哪怕用得很烂。不带字幕看剧对我帮助很大，强迫自己在网上跟陌生人聊天虽然吓人但很有效。你得接受一开始说得很笨拙，因为这就是变得流利的必经过程."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":0,"en":"Well, on the plus side, AI saves us a ton of time, like translating or summarising documents in seconds. It can also help doctors spot diseases earlier. But the worrying part is privacy and jobs, you know, some people might lose work. I use AI to fix my emails, but I try not to trust every answer it gives.","cn":"嗯，好处方面，AI帮我们省了大量时间，比如几秒钟翻译或总结文件。它还能帮医生更早发现疾病。但令人担忧的是隐私和就业问题，你知道，有些人可能会失业。我用AI改邮件，但我尽量不完全相信它给的每个答案."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":1,"en":"Definitely, and not just for tech people. Even as a regular user, you should understand what AI can and cannot do, so you do not get fooled by fake content. My dad is a teacher and he had to learn how to spot AI-written essays, because students were submitting them. So knowing the basics is almost a life skill now.","cn":"当然，而且不只是技术人员。即使作为普通用户，你也应该了解AI能做和不能做什么，这样才不会被假内容骗到。我爸是老师，他不得不学会分辨AI写的作文，因为学生开始交这种作业。所以懂点基础知识现在几乎是种生活技能."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":2,"en":"I think yes, but with guidance. Kids are already using it anyway, so banning it is pointless. The key is teaching them to use it as a tool, not a shortcut. My little brother uses it to explain hard maths steps, which is fine, but if he just copies answers, he learns nothing. So it really comes down to how it is used.","cn":"我觉得应该，但要有引导。孩子们反正已经在用了，禁掉也没用。关键在于教他们把AI当工具，而不是走捷径。我弟弟用它来解释难的数学步骤，这挺好的，但如果他只是抄答案，就什么都学不到。所以关键还是看怎么用."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":3,"en":"It helps in loads of little ways. Voice assistants set reminders, maps find the fastest route, and translation apps let me chat with tourists. In hospitals, AI can read scans faster than humans. I would say it mostly takes over the boring, repetitive stuff so we can focus on things that need real judgement.","cn":"它在很多小事上都帮得上忙。语音助手能设提醒，地图能找到最快路线，翻译软件让我能和游客聊天。在医院，AI读片比人还快。我觉得它主要是接管那些枯燥、重复的活儿，让我们能专注在真正需要判断力的事上."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":4,"en":"Honestly, yes, some of them are. I have seen classmates let AI write whole essays and then not even understand their own work. That is worrying. But it is not everyone, because plenty of students still use it just to check grammar or get ideas. So I would say there is a real risk, but it depends on the person.","cn":"说实话，有些人确实是。我见过同学让AI写整篇作文，然后连自己的作业都看不懂。这挺让人担心的。但也不是所有人，因为很多学生还是只把它用来检查语法或找思路。所以我觉得风险确实存在，但要看人."},{"b":"04","c":21,"i":5,"en":"I think teachers should design tasks that AI cannot fake easily, like in-class presentations or debates. That way students have to think for themselves. Also, talking openly about when AI use is cheating helps. My old teacher started asking us to explain our answers out loud, and that alone stopped a lot of copy-pasting.","cn":"我觉得老师应该设计AI不容易造假的任务，比如课堂演讲或辩论。这样学生就得自己思考。另外，坦诚讨论什么时候用AI算作弊也很有帮助。我以前有位老师开始要求我们大声解释答案，光这一招就制止了很多复制粘贴."},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":0,"en":"I think it is mostly about the excitement and the atmosphere. When you watch live, thousands of people are cheering and it feels electric. There is also the suspense, because anything can happen in real time. My friend is a huge football fan and he says watching a match live is completely different from seeing the score later online.","cn":"我觉得主要是那种激动和氛围。现场看的时候，成千上万人在欢呼，感觉特别带电。还有那种悬念感，因为实时比赛中什么都有可能发生。我朋友是个铁杆球迷，他说现场看比赛和事后在网上看比分完全是两码事."},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":1,"en":"Most people watch at home on the sofa, honestly, because it is cheap and comfortable. But plenty go to sports bars with friends, especially for big games, and the noise there is half the fun. Of course, if you are lucky enough to get tickets, watching in the stadium is the best, though it costs a fortune.","cn":"说实话，大多数人是在家沙发上看的，因为便宜又舒服。但也有很多人和朋友去体育酒吧，尤其是大赛的时候，那里的热闹本身就是一半的乐趣。当然，如果你运气好抢到票，在体育场看是最好的，不过那要花不少钱。"},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":2,"en":"The big advantage is flexibility. You can watch on your phone on the bus, pause it, replay the best bits, and it is basically free. You also get commentary in your own language. The downside is you miss the crowd and the shared excitement, which is a big part of why people love sport in the first place.","cn":"最大的好处是灵活。你能在公交上用手机看，可以暂停、重放最精彩的片段，而且基本免费。你还能听母语解说。缺点是你会错过人群和那种共享的激动，而这恰恰是人们喜欢体育的重要原因."},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":3,"en":"I would say team sports are great for kids, like football or basketball, because the atmosphere is lively and they can cheer along. Swimming or athletics meets are also good. I would avoid anything too violent, like boxing, because it is not really suitable for young children. It also depends on whether the kid can sit still for two hours.","cn":"我觉得团队运动很适合孩子，比如足球或篮球，因为氛围热闹，他们能一起加油。游泳或田径比赛也不错。我会避开太暴力的，比如拳击，因为它不太适合小孩子。这也取决于孩子能不能安静坐两个小时."},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":4,"en":"I think it is a mix of passion and the experience. For a real fan, seeing their team play in another country is a once-in-a-lifetime thing. It is also a good excuse for a holiday, you know, you combine travel with the game. My uncle flew abroad just to watch a Formula One race, and he still talks about it years later.","cn":"我觉得这是热爱和体验的结合。对真正的球迷来说，去别的国家看自己的球队比赛是一生一次的事。它也是个度假的好借口，你知道，把旅行和比赛结合起来。我叔叔专门飞去国外看F1比赛，多年以后他还老提起那次。"},{"b":"04","c":22,"i":5,"en":"In my country table tennis and badminton are hugely popular, partly because we are good at them and everyone can play. Basketball is massive among young people, and football has a big following too. Interestingly, the sports people watch are not always the ones they play, but those four are definitely the big ones here.","cn":"在我们国家，乒乓球和羽毛球特别受欢迎，部分原因是我们在这些项目上很强，而且人人都能玩。篮球在年轻人中非常火，足球也有大批粉丝。有意思的是，人们观看的运动不一定是他们自己玩的，但这四项在这里绝对是最火的。"},{"b":"04","c":23,"i":0,"en":"I think it is good, as long as it is a change you actually choose. Shaking up your routine can make you feel awake again, you know, like when I started jogging in the morning instead of scrolling my phone. But if it is forced on you, it can just feel stressful, so the reason behind the change really matters.","cn":"我觉得是好的，只要这个改变是你主动选的。打乱日常能让你重新感到清醒，比如我开始早上跑步代替刷手机。但如果是被迫的，就只会让人有压力，所以改变背后的原因真的很重要。"},{"b":"04","c":23,"i":1,"en":"It can be good, but it is risky. Switching jobs can boost your salary and give you fresh energy, like my cousin who moved to a start-up and grew fast. But if you jump too often, employers start to doubt you, and you never build deep skills. So I would say change is good when there is a clear reason, not just because you are bored.","cn":"这可能不错，但有风险。换工作能涨工资、带来新活力，比如我表哥跳槽到一家创业公司，成长很快。但如果跳得太频繁，雇主会开始怀疑你，而且你也积累不了深厚的技能。所以我觉得，有明确理由时改变是好的，而不是单纯因为厌倦。"},{"b":"04","c":23,"i":2,"en":"Generally yes, because a promotion usually means more money and more responsibility, and it can really boost your confidence. But it also comes with longer hours and bigger pressure, so some people actually dislike it. My friend got promoted and loved the title but hated the stress, so whether it is good depends on the person.","cn":"一般来说是好的，因为升职通常意味着更多收入和更多责任，还能大大提升自信。但它也伴随着更长的工时和更大的压力，所以有些人其实并不喜欢。我朋友升了职，喜欢那个头衔却讨厌那份压力，所以好不好要看人。"},{"b":"04","c":24,"i":0,"en":"I think it starts quite early, maybe around five or six, when kids begin to say what they like and dislike. My niece is six and she already insists on choosing her own clothes, which is a tiny opinion but still her own. Real, reasoned opinions probably come later, in the teenage years, when they start questioning what adults say.","cn":"我觉得开始得挺早的，大概五六岁，孩子开始表达喜欢和不喜欢的时候。我侄女六岁，已经坚持自己挑衣服了，虽然是很小的意见，但也是她自己的。真正有理有据的观点可能要晚些，到青少年时期，他们开始质疑大人说的话。"},{"b":"04","c":24,"i":1,"en":"I would say parents matter more when kids are young, because they are around all the time. You just trust what mum says. But as children grow up, teachers often carry more weight, because they seem like experts. My cousin used to argue with his mum using facts his physics teacher told him, which shows how much that shifted.","cn":"我觉得孩子小的时候，父母的影响更大，因为他们一直在身边。你就是相信妈妈说的话。但孩子长大后，老师往往更有分量，因为他们看起来像专家。我表弟以前会拿物理老师告诉他的事实去跟他妈争论，这说明这个转变有多大。"},{"b":"04","c":24,"i":2,"en":"Honestly, it depends on the child and the subject. In terms of time, kids actually spend more waking hours with teachers, so that adds up. But for personal things, like worries or feelings, they usually turn to parents, because parents feel safer. My little brother tells his teacher about homework but tells my mum everything else.","cn":"说实话，这要看孩子和具体事情。从时间上说，孩子醒着的时候其实跟老师待得更久，所以加起来不少。但在私人的事情上，比如烦恼或情绪，他们通常找父母，因为父母让人更有安全感。我弟弟会跟老师聊作业，但别的事都跟我妈说。"},{"b":"04","c":24,"i":3,"en":"I think young people mostly share opinions with close friends, because they are the same age and get it. Social media is huge too, so a lot of opinions get bounced around in group chats or online forums. With parents, they tend to share less, unless the topic is neutral like food or travel.","cn":"我觉得年轻人主要跟好朋友分享观点，因为同年龄，能互相理解。社交媒体也很重要，所以很多观点是在群聊或网络论坛里来回碰撞的。跟父母反而分享得少，除非话题比较中立，比如吃的或旅行。"},{"b":"04","c":24,"i":4,"en":"I think the most common ones are money plans, study plans and travel plans. People plan how to save for a house, what to study next, or where to go on holiday. Big life decisions like marriage or career changes get planned too, though usually with less detail than a trip.","cn":"我觉得最常见的计划是钱、学习和旅行。人们会规划怎么攒钱买房、接下来学什么、假期去哪。像结婚或转行这种人生大事也会做计划，只是通常没有旅行计划那么细。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":0,"en":"I think it happens a lot when the message is complicated or emotional. If someone has to think carefully about how to reply, they often delay. For example, my friend took three days to answer a job offer, because he had to weigh up the pros and cons. So a slow reply is not always rude, it can mean they care.","cn":"我觉得这种情况常常出现在信息比较复杂或涉及情绪的时候。如果别人得仔细想想怎么回，他们往往会拖。比如我朋友花三天才回复一个工作邀约，因为他得权衡利弊。所以回复慢不一定是不礼貌，有时反而说明他们在乎。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":1,"en":"Usually when they are busy at work or driving, or when it is late at night and they do not want to disturb anyone. Also, if the message is from someone annoying, some people just ignore it for a while. I have definitely done that, you know, put off replying to a sales message until I forgot about it completely.","cn":"通常是他们上班忙或者开车的时候，或者太晚了不想打扰别人。另外，如果信息是讨厌的人发的，有些人就先晾着。我肯定这么干过，比如把一条推销信息一直拖着，最后完全忘了。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":2,"en":"Honestly, I would wait a bit first, maybe a day, because people get busy. If it is urgent, I would just call instead of texting again. But if it is something personal and they still ignore me, I would probably send a light follow-up like hey, did you see my message, rather than getting annoyed straight away.","cn":"说实话，我会先等一等，也许等一天，因为人会忙。如果事情紧急，我就干脆打电话，而不是再发一条。但如果是很私人的事而他们还是不理我，我可能会轻松地追一句，比如你看到我消息了吗，而不是立刻生气。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":3,"en":"I think because it is less intrusive. With a message you can reply whenever you want, so you do not feel put on the spot. It also lets you think before you speak, which is great if it is something awkward. My mum always calls, though, so I guess it really depends on the person and the situation.","cn":"我觉得是因为发信息没那么打扰别人。发了消息，对方想什么时候回都行，不会觉得被逼着立刻回答。它还能让你说话前先想想，遇到尴尬的事就很好。不过我妈妈总是打电话，所以我觉得这真的要看人和情况。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":4,"en":"I try to be polite by starting with a greeting and using a friendly tone, and I avoid writing in all caps, since that looks like shouting. If it is someone older or a boss, I keep it more formal and add a thank you. Small things, but they really change how the message comes across.","cn":"我会尽量礼貌，开头先问个好、语气友好，也避免全用大写字母，因为那样看起来像在吼。如果对方是长辈或老板，我会写得更正式，还会加一句谢谢。都是小事，但真的会改变消息给人的感觉。"},{"b":"04","c":25,"i":5,"en":"I think it is because waiting feels like being ignored, and that hurts. Especially if you can see they are online, it feels like a tiny rejection. My sister gets really upset when her boyfriend takes hours to reply, even though he is just at work. So the anger is often about feeling unimportant, not the message itself.","cn":"我觉得是因为等待让人觉得被忽视了，而这很伤人。特别是如果你能看到对方在线，就会觉得像被小小地拒绝了。我姐姐会因为男朋友几小时不回而很生气，尽管他只是在上班。所以那种生气往往是源于觉得自己不重要，而不是消息本身。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":0,"en":"I think you need a mix of things, not one magic fix. Building more metro lines helps, but you also have to make buses and trains cheap and clean so people actually want to use them. Some cities charge drivers to enter the centre, which cuts traffic a lot. Honestly, changing people's habits is the hardest part.","cn":"我觉得需要多管齐下，没有什么神奇的单一办法。多修地铁有帮助，但你还得让公交和地铁便宜又干净，这样人们才愿意用。有些城市对进市中心的车辆收费，这能大大减少车流。说实话，改变人们的习惯才是最难的。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":1,"en":"It helps a lot, but it is not a cure on its own. If the metro is fast, cheap and goes where people need, plenty will leave the car at home, like in my city where the new line cut my commute in half. But if public transport is crowded and unreliable, people just go back to driving. So quality matters as much as quantity.","cn":"这帮助很大，但单靠它治不了根。如果地铁又快又便宜，而且通向人们需要的地方，很多人就会把车留在家里，比如在我所在的城市，新线路让我的通勤时间缩短了一半。但如果公共交通又挤又不可靠，人们就会重新开车。所以质量和数量一样重要。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":2,"en":"Yes, to some extent. Highways can move traffic faster, and ring roads keep big trucks out of the city centre, which helps. But there is a catch, because wider roads sometimes encourage more people to drive, so the jam just comes back. I have seen this myself, a new road near us got busy within a year.","cn":"在某种程度上是的。高速路能让车流更快，环路能把大卡车挡在市中心外，这有帮助。但有个陷阱，因为更宽的路有时会鼓励更多人来开车，于是堵车又回来了。我自己就见过，我们家附近新修的路一年内就变得很堵了。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":3,"en":"I think smart traffic lights that adjust to real traffic are a good start, because they stop one empty lane getting a long green light. Banning cars on certain days based on number plates also works in some cities. And honestly, encouraging people to work from home a couple of days a week takes a lot of pressure off the roads.","cn":"我觉得能根据实时车流调整的智能红绿灯是个好开头，因为它们能避免一条空车道却一直绿灯。有些城市按车牌限号也挺有效。而且说实话，鼓励人们每周在家工作几天，能给道路减轻不少压力。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":4,"en":"Yes, I do, mostly the subway, because it is fast and I can read on the way. I do not miss sitting in traffic or hunting for a parking space. The only downside is rush hour, when it is so packed you can barely move. But overall it is cheaper and less stressful than driving, so I am happy with it.","cn":"是的，我经常坐，主要是地铁，因为它快，我还能在路上看书。我不怀念堵在路上或者到处找车位。唯一的缺点就是高峰期，挤得几乎动不了。但总体来说它比开车便宜、压力也小，所以我挺满意的。"},{"b":"04","c":26,"i":5,"en":"I would rather be on a bus, honestly, because at least I can stand up, stretch, or read a book. In a car you are stuck in one seat, and if you are driving, you cannot even relax. That said, if the bus is packed and hot, a comfy car with air-con and good music would win, so it depends on the conditions.","cn":"说实话，我宁愿坐公交，因为至少我能站起来、伸伸腿，或者看会儿书。在车里你被卡在一个座位上，如果是开车，连放松都做不到。话虽如此，如果公交又挤又热，那还是一辆有空调、有好音乐的舒服轿车更胜一筹，所以要看具体情况。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":0,"en":"Yes, I think people here really love going out in their free time. Weekend parks and shopping streets are always packed. Even when money is tight, families still go for a walk, have a picnic or grab street food, because it is cheap. Staying home all weekend would feel quite boring to most people I know.","cn":"是的，我觉得这里的人真的很喜欢在空闲时出去。周末的公园和商业街总是人山人海。就算手头不宽裕，一家人还是会去散散步、野餐或买点小吃，因为便宜。就我认识的大多数人来说，整个周末宅在家会觉得很无聊。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":1,"en":"I would say it is a real mix. Older people might go for a walk or meet friends for tea, while young people prefer movies, cafes or shopping. Sport is big too, like badminton or hiking. My family usually has a long lunch together on Sundays, which is our way of spending leisure time. So it is quite varied.","cn":"我觉得真是五花八门。年纪大的人可能去散步或约朋友喝茶，年轻人则喜欢看电影、泡咖啡馆或逛街。运动也很流行，比如羽毛球或徒步。我家通常在周日一起吃顿长长的午饭，这就是我们度过空闲时间的方式。所以形式挺多样的。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":2,"en":"It has changed things a lot. On the one hand, streaming and games mean people can relax at home without going anywhere. On the other hand, apps help us find new places to eat or travel. My sister plans whole weekends just from short videos on her phone. So technology can keep people in, but it can also push them out.","cn":"它改变了很多。一方面，流媒体和游戏意味着人们足不出户就能在家放松。另一方面，各种应用帮我们找到新餐厅或新地方去旅行。我姐姐光靠手机上的短视频就能规划整个周末。所以科技既能让人宅在家，也能把人拉出门。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":3,"en":"No, not at all. Young people, students especially, have stacks of free time, sometimes too much. The problem is more about money than time for some of them. And working parents are often the ones with the least leisure, because they are juggling jobs and kids. So age is not the deciding factor here.","cn":"完全不是这样。年轻人，尤其是学生，有大把空闲时间，有时甚至太多了。对其中一些人来说，问题更多是钱而不是时间。而那些有工作的父母往往是空闲最少的，因为他们要兼顾工作和孩子。所以年龄不是决定因素。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":4,"en":"I think because it gives people something to look forward to. A day off breaks the routine and lets you recharge, so you come back to work with more energy. It is also a chance to see family or just do nothing for a while. Honestly, without days off I think most people would burn out pretty quickly.","cn":"我觉得是因为它让人有盼头。休息日打破日常，让你充电，回来工作时更有精力。它也是见家人或者干脆放空一会儿的机会。说实话，没有休息日的话，我觉得大多数人很快就累垮了。"},{"b":"04","c":27,"i":5,"en":"I know, right, holidays can be exhausting, with the packing, the delays and the crowds. But I think people still go because the good memories outweigh the hassle. You get a change of scenery and time with the people you love. For me, even a tiring trip is worth it if I come back feeling like I actually lived a bit.","cn":"是啊，假期确实能让人累垮，打包、延误还有人山人海。但我觉得人们还是去，是因为美好的回忆盖过了那些麻烦。你能换个环境，和你爱的人待在一起。对我来说，只要回来时感觉自己真的好好活过一阵，再累的旅行也值得。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":0,"en":"I think the best way is to give kids small routines early, like packing their own school bag the night before. If parents do everything for them, they never learn. Also, keeping a simple family calendar on the fridge really helps, so the child can see what is coming up. It teaches them to plan without being nagged.","cn":"我觉得最好的办法是早早给孩子建立小习惯，比如头天晚上自己收拾书包。如果父母什么都替他们做，他们永远学不会。另外，在冰箱上贴一个简单的家庭日历很有用，这样孩子能看到接下来有什么事。它能在不被唠叨的情况下教会他们做规划。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":1,"en":"Definitely when there is a deadline or a big event, like exams, weddings or moving house. Travel is another one, because if you are not organized you miss a flight. At work too, if you have a presentation, being organized means you do not panic at the last minute. Basically, any situation where mistakes cost time or money.","cn":"当然是有截止日期或大事的时候，比如考试、婚礼或搬家。旅行也是，因为如果不安排妥当，你就会误机。工作上也是，如果要做演讲，有条理就意味着你不会在最后一刻慌神。基本上，任何出错会浪费时间或金钱的场合都需要。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":2,"en":"I would say no, not everything needs a plan. If you over-prepare every little thing, you lose the fun and spontaneity. Sometimes just wandering around a new city is the best part of a trip. But for things that matter, like a job interview or a wedding, yes, you should prepare properly. So it is about knowing what deserves the effort.","cn":"我觉得不是所有事都需要计划。如果你把每件小事都过度准备，就失去了乐趣和随性。有时候在新城市里随便逛逛才是旅行最棒的部分。但对于重要的事，比如面试或婚礼，那就该好好准备。所以关键是要知道哪些事值得花那份功夫。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":3,"en":"Often yes, especially for big events, because no one person can handle everything alone. When I helped organize a class trip, we split the work, one person booked the bus, another sorted the food. That said, for small personal things you can usually just do it yourself. So it depends on the scale of the event.","cn":"通常是的，尤其是大型活动，因为没有人能独自应付所有事。我帮忙组织班级旅行时，我们把活儿分开，一个人订车，另一个人管吃的。话虽如此，小事的话你自己通常就能搞定。所以这要看活动的规模。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":4,"en":"I feel quite anxious and a bit rushed, honestly. Your heart races and you keep thinking you have forgotten something. I once showed up to a meeting without my notes and felt terrible the whole time. It taught me that a bit of preparation is worth the effort, because the stress of being unprepared is way worse.","cn":"说实话，我会很焦虑，还有点手忙脚乱。心跳加速，老觉得忘了什么东西。我有一次开会没带资料，整个过程都感觉很糟糕。这让我明白，一点准备是值得的，因为没准备好的那种压力要糟得多。"},{"b":"04","c":28,"i":5,"en":"I think it depends on my mood and the event. For something I care about, like a friend's birthday, I actually enjoy organising it and getting the details right. But for a random party, I would rather just show up and enjoy it. So I like a bit of both, preparing when it matters and relaxing when it does not.","cn":"我觉得这要看我的心情和活动本身。对于我在意的事，比如朋友的生日，我其实很享受去组织、把细节弄好。但对于一个随便的聚会，我宁愿直接去玩。所以我两种都喜欢，重要的事做准备，不重要的事就放松。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":0,"en":"I think food is deeply tied to culture, so the differences are big. In my country a special meal is often about sharing, like a whole fish or hotpot in the middle of the table, whereas in many Western countries it is more about individual plates. Also, we value how dishes balance each other, not just the taste of one thing.","cn":"我觉得食物和文化的联系非常紧密，所以差异很大。在我们国家，一顿特别的饭往往是关于分享，比如桌子中间摆一整条鱼或火锅，而在很多西方国家，更多是各吃各的。另外，我们看重菜和菜之间怎么相互平衡，而不只是某一道菜的味道。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":1,"en":"Yes, definitely. Dumplings at Chinese New Year, rice cakes, and mooncakes at Mid-Autumn are the obvious ones. Each has a meaning, like round shapes standing for family reunion. My grandma always makes dumplings on New Year's Eve, and we all help, so it is half about the food and half about being together.","cn":"当然有。春节吃饺子、年糕，中秋吃月饼，都是最典型的。每样都有寓意，比如圆形代表团圆。我奶奶总是在除夕包饺子，我们都帮忙，所以这既是为了吃，也是为了团聚。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":2,"en":"I think it is partly about showing love and respect, and partly about the occasion itself. On a birthday or anniversary, an expensive meal says you matter to me. There is also a bit of pressure, you know, if you pick a cheap place people might judge you. So it is a mix of emotion and social expectation.","cn":"我觉得一方面是表达爱和尊重，另一方面是场合本身。在生日或纪念日，一顿贵的饭等于说，你对我来说很重要。也有一点压力，你知道，如果你挑个便宜的地方，别人可能会说闲话。所以这是情感和社会期待的结合。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":3,"en":"Yes, I really do. Eating together is one of the few times everyone is actually sitting face to face without a screen. We talk about our day and it keeps us close. At my home, dinner is when my parents ask about school, and honestly some of our best conversations happen there. So I think it matters a lot.","cn":"是的，我非常认同。一起吃饭是少数几个大家真正面对面、没有屏幕的时刻。我们聊聊各自的一天，这让我们更亲近。在我家，晚饭时父母会问学校的事，说实话我们最好的一些对话就发生在那里。所以我觉这非常重要。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":4,"en":"Well, going back in time, family meals used to be much more disciplined, like no talking until the elders started. There was more ritual and hierarchy around eating together. Now it is more relaxed and casual. My grandpa often talks about how silent the dinner table was when he was a kid, which is hard to imagine today.","cn":"嗯，回到过去的话，家庭聚餐以前规矩多得多，比如长辈不动筷就不能说话。那时一起吃饭有更多的礼数和等级。现在则更随意放松了。我爷爷常说起他小时候饭桌上有多安静，这在今天很难想象。"},{"b":"04","c":29,"i":5,"en":"Honestly, I think it is a bit rude if everyone is doing it the whole time. It sends the message that your phone is more interesting than the person in front of you. That said, if it is a quick check for a message or taking a photo of the food, that is fine. It is really about striking a balance and not ignoring your family.","cn":"说实话，如果所有人都一直看手机，我觉得有点没礼貌。它传递的信息是，手机比坐在你面前的人更有趣。话虽如此，如果只是快速看一眼消息，或给菜拍张照，那没问题。关键是要把握好分寸，不要冷落了家人。"}];